WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.840
Music man, lead us off.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.450
Hello and welcome to this
MIT virtual town hall.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.650
And thank you, Marty Marks, of
MIT's Music and Theater Arts

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.150
section for that wonderful
opening music from Jerome Kern.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.910
I'm Alfred Ironside, vice
president for Communications,

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:30.840
welcoming you to the second
in a series of gatherings

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.550
across our community.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:36.030
In a moment, I'll hand off to
Rafael Reif, president of MIT,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:40.220
and our outstanding panel
of faculty and staff.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.790
But first, a few reminders
on accessibility.

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.010
We are live
captioning this event

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:50.660
and also have simultaneous
sign language translation.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.730
Notices for both were
sent out in advance.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.450
We also have an
audio-only call in line

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.300
for those without a strong
internet connection.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.970
And this event is
being recorded and will

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:06.800
be available at this
URL for viewing anytime.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.930
Now to our format.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.810
As with our first
town hall, we have

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.140
gathered some of your
questions in advance,

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.560
but this time, we will also be
responding to your questions

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.680
live, as many as we can get to.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.950
You can submit questions
through the Q&amp;A feature

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.650
at the bottom of your screen.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.010
Our purpose today is
to offer an update

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.910
to share what we know about
the complex factors shaping

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.910
the decisions ahead
of MIT and there are

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.340
a lot of factors to talk about.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.540
So let's begin.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:48.570
With pleasure, I pass the floor
to MIT President Rafael Reif.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:53.610
Thank you, Alfred, and to
our students, and parents,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.590
and staff, and faculty, alumni--

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.680
warm greetings from Cambridge.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.260
Actually to be more precise,
warm greetings from Gray House.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.080
We are all members
of the MIT community.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.340
And that means we have a
lot of things in common.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:16.380
Among those, we care for one
another and we care for MIT.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.610
COVID-19 has affected and
it's affecting all of us.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.330
But it's affecting some
much harder than others.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.500
So as we work on solutions
with the challenge of COVID-19

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.670
on our campus, let's
recognize, let's

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:35.770
be sensitive that each of
us is experiencing COVID-19

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.130
differently.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.470
And each of us had
different points of view.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.590
So let's respect
those differences

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.080
and let's show empathy
for those who are going

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.550
through a very difficult time.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:52.150
And let's find solutions that
work for our whole community.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.520
We had closed our campus
about eight weeks ago or so.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.480
Four weeks ago, we had
our first town hall.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.670
Since that town hall,
we updated the community

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.140
on our financial situation.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.700
We also shared some
decisions on the summer.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.610
Today's town hall is not to
share additional decisions.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:16.600
Actually, today's
town hall is mostly

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.610
to share our thinking about
decisions we have yet to meet.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:27.400
And top of that list is when and
how we will reopen the campus.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.460
We have not made
that decision yet.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.760
We have many people from all
parts of the campus working

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.580
together to come up
with scenarios on

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:40.210
how we can reopen
the campus and when.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:44.200
All these individuals are
organized in working groups,

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.360
and the leaders of all
those working groups

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.180
are with us today to
share their thinking

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.160
and to answer your questions.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.810
In addition to
them, we also have

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.370
members of the
central administration

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:58.430
that will be available to
answer your questions, as well.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.750
One of the most
important issues that we

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:05.700
have to consider before deciding
how and when to open the campus

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.540
is the state of the
public health situation

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.160
and the state of
medical science.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.280
And for that, we have
a terrific guest today,

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.720
a person that I deeply admire.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.645
An MIT professor
of the practice who

00:05:19.645 --> 00:05:23.620
is also the director of the
Reagan Institute of MTH, MIT,

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:24.640
and Harvard.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.400
And this is Dr. Bruce Walker.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.610
He is on the frontlines of
much of the research going

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.990
on these days to
overcome COVID-19

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.420
and he is a terrific
advisor to all of us.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.830
Bruce, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.260
Happy to be here.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.940
Bruce, the picture
is so complex.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.630
I would like, if you can,
to just clarify it for us.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.440
So before we open
the campus, we have

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.220
to pay attention to social
distancing and testing

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:58.520
and tracing and
medical treatments,

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.070
perhaps even vaccines.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.530
What can you tell us
about the landscape?

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.510
How would you advise us to
consider the landscape in terms

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.075
of reopening the campus?

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.840
There's an experiment
going on right

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.550
now that I think is giving us
knowledge that helps direct

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:20.900
those sorts of decisions.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.780
And that experiment is
happening in hospitals

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.760
across the country.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:33.200
Mass General is one example,
where I work as a clinician.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.690
And the reality is that using
personal protective equipment

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:46.520
and monitoring for symptoms
has resulted in health workers

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.620
actually being able to be safe.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:50.120
The problems for
health care workers

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.240
have come when they
haven't had masks to use

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.580
and they haven't had the
additional personal protective

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:59.030
equipment when they're
interfacing directly

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.740
with patients.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.850
I think the emerging
data suggests

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:08.600
that if you wear a mask and
everybody else wears a mask,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:13.950
the chances of transmission
drop dramatically.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.790
I think if you add to
that frequent temperature

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:21.170
monitoring, the chances
of transmission drop

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:24.230
even more, because you can
identify people with a fever

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:28.400
right away, and remove them
then from the community.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:34.700
And so what we need to think
about is what resources

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:36.200
do we have right
now, what knowledge

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.867
do we have right now
that might guide us

00:07:37.867 --> 00:07:40.890
and I think the knowledge we
have is a suggestion that--

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:45.080
a pretty firm suggestion-- that
the judicious use of masks,

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:50.480
and social distancing
or physical distancing,

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:53.450
and symptom and
temperature monitoring

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:57.170
will have a dramatic
impact on transmissibility.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:05.600
As you probably are
guessing or knowing,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.110
there is tremendous anxiety on
when we will open the campus.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:14.180
And for that, we need to use
the information you told us.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.980
At the same time,
we also have to try

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:20.450
to imagine and have a
crystal ball on what little

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:23.040
we know that is
different from today

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.510
perhaps four weeks from now,
perhaps eight weeks from now.

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:29.090
If we will know
nothing different,

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.450
then we might as well
make a decision right now.

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:36.440
Things are changing-- what
can you tell us about that?

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:38.450
I'm not suggesting you
can tell us exactly

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:39.559
what's going to happen--

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:41.299
nobody has a crystal ball--

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:42.950
or perhaps you do.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.590
But my main point is, what
do you think might change?

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:49.520
Why is that we probably
should wait as long as we can

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.490
as opposed to deciding today?

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:55.190
I think there are
a couple of things

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:58.520
that will become apparent.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:01.430
One of them is
surveys to understand

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.860
just how many people
have been infected

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:08.300
and just how close to
herd immunity we may be.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.220
Herd immunity is
when enough people

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:14.150
have been exposed that
the likelihood of one

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:18.650
person encountering
an uninfected person

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:23.270
diminishes to the point where
ongoing transmissions become

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:24.800
less.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.620
We don't yet know if
antibodies are protective,

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:31.910
but there's reason to
assume that those are.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:36.410
And we'll know more about that
from animal experiments that

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:40.700
are ongoing right now I think
that will help to guide that.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.580
But I think we'll begin
to get a sense of just

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.820
how widespread the
infection has been.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:49.790
And I think that that'll be
important information for us

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.480
to understand.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:55.650
I think also, as tests
become more available,

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:58.910
we'll be better able
to monitor for example

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:02.840
the whole population at MIT
to when students come back

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:04.080
to follow them.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.930
I think the other
really important thing

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:07.970
that's a little bit
further on the horizon

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:12.410
is the development of high
throughput, inexpensive,

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.620
saliva-based testing
for virus itself.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:20.630
And we sponsored a
webinar on Friday

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:23.120
that I'd love to
send out and make

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.840
available to the people on
this town hall to watch.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.940
There are some
extraordinary advances

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:33.320
being made by professors at
MIT and their graduate students

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:41.150
and postdocs that give me
reason to think that it's

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:45.802
within the foreseeable
future that you can get up

00:10:45.802 --> 00:10:48.260
in the morning, and brush your
teeth, and at the same time,

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:51.440
take a Q-Tip and swirl it
around your [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:54.290
and stick it in a tube, and
have it turn a color if you're

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:58.220
infected and not turn
color if you're not.

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.080
We will get out of
this predicament

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:03.950
that we're in because of science
and engineering and technology.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:10.530
And MIT is the focal point of
that in the world, in my view.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:12.800
And when you watch
what is happening

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.110
on this on this
webinar from people

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:21.830
like Feng Jiang
and others, you'll

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:24.940
have reason to be hopeful.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.200
Thank you so very
much for joining us.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:28.240
I really appreciate it.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.670
And you have always been
available to us to advise us.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:34.140
And we will keep
abusing your time for us

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:35.335
as long as you let us.

00:11:35.335 --> 00:11:36.730
Thank you so much.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:37.750
You're welcome.

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:40.150
I think we're going
to move now to what

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:42.050
I mentioned at the beginning.

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:45.700
We have different working
groups working together

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:49.180
to try to come up with
scenarios for us for the fall.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.880
We have working groups
on residential life,

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:56.230
on academic, on research, on the
use of physical space and staff

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.060
and community.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:00.400
There appear to be
differing, but the truth

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:02.980
is that all those
recommendations, everything

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.103
they're coming up with,
really, those elements are

00:12:06.103 --> 00:12:07.270
intertwined with each other.

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:10.030
So we really have to have not
just working on those areas,

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.917
but working them
together, which is

00:12:12.917 --> 00:12:14.500
why we're going to
just introduce them

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.308
right now jointly, and
ask a few questions.

00:12:17.308 --> 00:12:19.600
And then they'll be ready to
answer your own questions.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:23.010
So let's start with
our [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:26.710
And that's for Suzy Nelson to
start with, our vice president

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:28.750
and being for student life.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:33.100
Suzy, thank you for
joining us today.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.520
I hear from you and others
that the number one challenge

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:40.300
you're facing,
we're all facing, is

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.100
how to repopulate the campus.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:45.490
And that includes how to
bring stuff back, the faculty,

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:48.550
the students, and
postdocs, and so forth.

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:50.350
Your big responsibility
is, of course,

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.110
students, and dorms
or residence halls.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.780
So what are your views?

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:58.990
What are you thinking about
repopulating this fall when

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:02.050
it comes to residential areas?

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.010
Thank you, Rafael.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:03.820
Hi, everyone.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:06.520
Thanks so much
for joining today.

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:08.890
Let me just provide a
little bit of context,

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:13.240
because we actually have 2,000
people living in residence now.

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:18.610
We have 1,300 graduate students,
and 200 undergraduate students,

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:22.180
and around 500 partners,
and spouses, and children,

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.620
and house team members.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:28.480
And so as we look to
the fall, I will say,

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:30.160
we've been thinking
really hard on this,

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:33.220
but we still have a lot
more questions than answers.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:36.130
And the main point that
I want to make today

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:39.490
is that we really want students
to help us problem solve.

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:42.010
And there's a few ways
that they can do that.

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:46.180
The first is we have been
sending out some surveys

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:50.080
to those who live in residence
and those who live off campus.

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:52.907
And we've been asking you to
tell us about your experience.

00:13:52.907 --> 00:13:55.240
And that really helps us think
about what the fall might

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:59.840
look like, having a good sense
of how it is for you right now.

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:04.510
And the second is yesterday,
we invited all students

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:08.560
to take a look at a problem
statement that's online.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:12.770
And we created a We
Solve for Fall idea bank,

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:14.890
and we would really like
you to engage with us

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:17.090
all the questions that we have.

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:18.790
There's a lot of
resources there,

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:22.960
some that Bruce just mentioned,
that you could take a look at.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:25.850
But we would like you to
engage with us in that way.

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:29.500
And we also invite you to
join one of too many townhall

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:33.010
meetings that we will have
after this big townhall.

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:36.340
Those will be held
on Wednesday, May 13.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:38.150
One will be for
graduate students

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:40.780
And the other will be
for undergraduates.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:42.218
They will be hosted
by our student

00:14:42.218 --> 00:14:43.510
leaders and our heads of house.

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:45.610
So please look for those
details and we really

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:47.680
hope that you'll attend.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.800
And then finally, as we
think about these problems,

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:55.280
I think they can be framed
in really two goals.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:57.970
The first goal is
that we know we

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:00.460
will have some new constraints.

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:04.210
But how can we look at
this in a creative way

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:07.600
and really reimagine
the MIT campus

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:10.090
so that it's
positive, and special,

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:12.760
and as intellectually
and personally engaging

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:15.460
as we want it to be.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:19.600
And then the second big goal
is as we make these decisions,

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:24.610
how can we be sure we prioritize
community health and well-being

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:26.710
for all who are here on campus.

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:28.480
So within these
two main goals, we

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:33.570
have some considerations that I
think students can think about

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:35.890
and what all of us
can think about.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.250
What values should play
into these decisions

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:41.530
and how should we make
these housing assignments

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:43.690
for our students
when they return.

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:46.210
What strategies will
be the most effective

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.850
as we try to manage
the spread of the virus

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.950
and keep our community
healthy, especially

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:52.780
in our residence halls.

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.360
And then finally,
how do we get buy in

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:57.310
and how do we
communicate effectively

00:15:57.310 --> 00:16:01.180
about these new ideas so
that they can be implemented

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:03.520
with some effectiveness.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:05.860
We're really
fortunate because we

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:08.560
have faculty who
live in our houses

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:11.020
and I'm very happy
that two heads of house

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:12.970
are joining us today.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:15.040
And they have a
lot of experience

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.810
certainly as professors,
but they also

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:19.790
have experience living in
residence with our students.

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:22.030
So I want to give them
a moment to talk as well

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.980
about what they're seeing
in the residence halls

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:26.660
and how they view this.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:29.900
David Singer, he's our
department head and professor

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:32.900
of political science, and
he lives in MacGregor House

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:35.060
in undergraduate community.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:38.750
And Julie Shah is a
professor in AeroAstro,

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:42.050
and she's the head of house
in Sydney Pacific, which is

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:44.330
a graduate student community.

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:47.180
So David, let's
turn to you first.

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:49.850
Could you offer some
thoughts on what

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:52.810
you think about what's
before us this fall?

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:54.410
Again, thank you, Suzy.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:55.810
Happy to be here.

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:57.560
When we think about
Suzy's these questions

00:16:57.560 --> 00:16:59.840
in the context of the
undergraduate dorms,

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:02.090
two considerations jump out.

00:17:02.090 --> 00:17:05.010
First is that every
dorm is different.

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:08.457
They range in size from
93 to 500 residents.

00:17:08.457 --> 00:17:10.040
And they have different
configurations

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:14.420
of rooms, bathrooms, kitchens,
lounges, dining halls,

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.310
and common spaces.

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:19.280
And second, each dorm has its
own form of student government

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:23.210
that serves as a Steward of
that community's traditions.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:26.210
So I think the way forward
in the coming weeks

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:29.690
and months will be for each
residential community to think

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:33.770
about how students
circulate in each community,

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:37.010
to think about how to
foster social interactions

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:38.270
and in a safe manner.

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:42.320
And that we're partnering
with modeling experts

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.720
from IDSS and other
units, and we're listening

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:47.150
to experts like Dr. Walker.

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.700
And for the students
in each community

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.640
to take the lead in
designing new guidelines

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:55.880
that respect the unique
physical and social features

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.250
of each community and
that also, importantly,

00:17:58.250 --> 00:18:01.370
respect the safety of the
more vulnerable members

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:02.720
of each community.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:05.980
And this is MIT, so the outcome
will be a new traditions.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:07.880
It will hopefully
be clever strategies

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:09.680
that no one's thought of before.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:12.080
And importantly,
continuity in some

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:15.480
of the core aspects of
residential life that make

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:19.080
MIT a special place
to live and to study.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:20.130
Great.

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:21.780
Thank you, David.

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:24.365
Julie, what are some of
your observations as head

00:18:24.365 --> 00:18:25.490
of house in Sydney Pacific?

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:29.810
Thank you, Suzy.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.880
So I'm currently in a dorm
with more than 400 students,

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:35.790
and I can say that Sydney
Pacific exemplifies

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:39.720
what a thriving campus community
looks like, despite these very

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.460
challenging circumstances.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:43.650
We have a strong
community in SP,

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.550
but frankly, our
community does seem

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:47.820
to depend on
physical gatherings.

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:49.475
And back in March,
I will admit, I

00:18:49.475 --> 00:18:51.600
did worry for a moment that
social distancing would

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:55.237
bring everything to a
halt. And that was silly.

00:18:55.237 --> 00:18:56.820
You know, we have
weekly movie nights,

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:59.040
game made, virtual dinner
series, group coloring

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.490
sessions, book clubs, and
many other events continue on,

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:05.160
both for those that are
on campus and off campus.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:08.490
And it's small efforts to make
each other feel heard and cared

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:10.560
for that have gone a long way.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:13.030
Our students are not only
coping, but they're innovating.

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:15.810
So seemingly overnight, they
mobilized the collaboration

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:18.210
with MIT Medical to implement
suppression measures

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:19.750
against the coronavirus.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:23.250
The effort was actually sparked
by two questions in the dorm--

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:25.470
can we understand utilization
of our common spaces

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.340
in the dorms like common
kitchens, bathrooms, laundry,

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:31.773
and the risk of exposure to
the virus in these places?

00:19:31.773 --> 00:19:33.690
Grad students are now
working with Lincoln Lab

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:36.840
to help test and improve
privacy-preserving mobile apps

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:39.180
that may help in
alerting residents

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:41.010
of potential exposures.

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:42.180
Thanks to our students.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:45.120
There'll be ways for more
to get involved soon.

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:47.910
And it's really all very
MIT and it's something

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:51.090
to make us all very proud.

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:53.440
Thanks, David and thanks, Julie.

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:55.350
Those are great
thoughts and comments.

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:57.720
And again, we really
appreciate your partnership

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.910
and we are looking forward
to working together

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:01.480
with the students.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:06.210
So please go to the
Idea Bank and also,

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:09.780
join us for the many townhalls,
which will be next week.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:12.210
Rafael, I'll pass
it back to you.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:13.000
Thank you, Suzy.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.480
Thank you very
much for everything

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.390
you do with so much
care for us student.

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:20.740
And thank you, David and
Julie, for joining us, as well.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:22.950
Moving on to
academic continuity.

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:25.800
And that working group
is led by Ian Waitz,

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:28.060
our vice chancellor for
undergraduate and graduate

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:29.820
application.

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:32.090
Ian, can you tell
us a little bit

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:33.740
about the scenarios
you're thinking?

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:37.940
How are you beginning to
envision options for us

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:39.960
for the fall?

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:41.180
Thank you, Rafael.

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:45.440
So I would say first,
at an individual level,

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:50.540
we're anticipating that
we will have staff members

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:54.770
faculty, students
who either may be ill

00:20:54.770 --> 00:20:59.930
or may be high risk individuals
so that we can have situations

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:02.090
where we have
faculty who can't be

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:04.310
on the physical campus
and students who can

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:06.170
and vice versa.

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:09.500
So we have to be
two-way remote capable

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:11.750
for much of our curriculum.

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:14.200
That's a heavy lift.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:17.510
But we're preparing to do that.

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:19.730
The bigger question that
I think a lot of people

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:23.480
are interested in is what will
the fall look like in terms

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:25.910
of how much online
education will we have,

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:28.910
and how much in-person
education will we have,

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:30.650
and what does it
mean to be socially

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:33.980
distanced in our instructional
spaces, and labs, and project

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:37.210
spaces, and classrooms,
and things like that.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:40.130
And as I listen to
Bruce, it's pretty clear

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:44.450
that things are changing so
rapidly that one of the best

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:48.020
things that we can do is
plan for multiple different

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:52.160
scenarios very, very vigorously,
and prepare for those,

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:55.760
but wait to make decisions
until we learn more.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:57.560
So we're planning
for things that

00:21:57.560 --> 00:22:03.260
go all the way from a
fully online fall to having

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:06.500
all of the students back, to
things that are in between--

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:09.680
half the students
for half the time.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:13.010
And I, at least as
an optimist at heart,

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:16.820
am very hopeful that we will
have at least some students

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:19.370
on campus in the fall.

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:23.260
But it's constrained, obviously,
by a social distancing

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:26.460
and by testing methodologies
and things like that.

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:31.220
So we're still working with a
range of different scenarios

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:35.840
so that we can be prepared as we
learn more in the weeks ahead.

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:38.300
There's so many people
that are working on this.

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:41.090
I want to highlight a sort
of a couple individuals

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:42.680
who've been very
helpful in thinking

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.600
through different
aspects of this.

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:46.970
One is Rick Danheiser,
the chair of the faculty,

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:53.180
who has just in a wonderfully
thoughtful way, helped

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:55.940
reimagine and
rebuild the policies

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:58.010
as we go through
this whole process.

00:22:58.010 --> 00:22:59.990
And so I'll first
turn to Rick and ask

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:03.190
if he can comment a little
bit about all of that,

00:23:03.190 --> 00:23:05.930
because it's just an important
part of moving forward

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:07.140
in whatever we do.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:08.580
Rick?

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:09.570
Thanks, Ian.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:11.440
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:14.130
As I described in an email
letter to the community

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:18.090
just this past weekend, I'm
leading the academic policy

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:22.920
and regulations team we call
APART, of faculty and students,

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:25.050
which the faculty
officers created

00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:28.230
in mid-April as a
successor to the emergency

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:31.575
academic regulations
or EARS team.

00:23:31.575 --> 00:23:33.450
You may recall that it
was the EARS team that

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:36.660
developed those emergency
regulations, including

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:39.210
the pass/no record
grading scheme

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:41.310
in effect for the spring.

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:44.190
So as we formulate the
policies associated

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:47.130
with our academic
program for this summer

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:50.130
and that will apply in
the fall, the APART team

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:52.750
has been guided by
several principles.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:56.850
First and foremost, our
aim is to develop policies

00:23:56.850 --> 00:24:00.210
that will promote and support
an educational experience

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:03.150
for undergraduate
and graduate students

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:07.800
that is as full, as enriching,
and as rewarding as possible.

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:10.800
At the same time, we
strive to recognize

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:14.280
and be sensitive to the fact
that individual students

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:17.400
and individual
instructors may be facing

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:21.060
very different challenges
and obstacles as we navigate

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:24.330
our way through the
current emergency.

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:27.780
As one example, this week,
the academic policy team

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:31.980
is working to finalize a
plan for student and subject

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:33.150
surveys.

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:34.920
From the outset,
the team recognized

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:39.900
that the usual numerical subject
evaluation system was not

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:44.160
appropriate for this unusual
semester in which instructors

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:47.250
in many cases had less
than two weeks to jury

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:49.670
rig a remote version
of their subjects--

00:24:49.670 --> 00:24:52.440
sometimes while
simultaneously dealing

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:56.370
with health and personal
challenges of their own.

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:59.160
At the same time,
APART appreciates

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:03.000
that we have a responsibility to
gather feedback from and listen

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:04.320
to our students.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:06.510
And we do not want to
lose the opportunity

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:09.600
to gather valuable
input and advice on how

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:11.610
to improve our core
subjects, should it

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:15.490
be necessary to offer remote
versions again in the future.

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:17.430
So with the help of
advice from students

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:20.820
and the expertise of the MIT
Teaching and Learning Lab,

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:24.990
I believe that the system we
are developing and will announce

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:27.480
hopefully within a week
will achieve these goals

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:29.040
and prove valuable
to the community

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:33.660
as we plot our course
forward in the future, Ian.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:34.950
Thanks, Rick.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:38.160
Just one of dozens
and dozens of examples

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:41.760
where Rick and his team,
have worked through faculty

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:44.640
committees and engaging
students and things, to sort of

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:47.460
figure out how best we
can plot the way forward.

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:50.940
I'll also note that Rick is
one of the faculty members

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:55.050
who had two weeks to spin
his class up on its head,

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:57.060
and jury rig it,
and make it work.

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:01.390
So he also has some personal
experience with that.

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:05.680
Both David and Julie mentioned
that so much is happening,

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:07.620
it's just so very MIT--

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:09.622
Bruce Walker did, as well.

00:26:09.622 --> 00:26:11.580
One of the things that
will happen in the fall,

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.800
no matter what
scenario we have, is

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:18.040
that they're going to be a
lot of very MIT experiences.

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:21.750
And one of the people who has
been helping to imagine those

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:25.005
is associate dean of
engineering, Peko Hosoi.

00:26:25.005 --> 00:26:29.700
So I'm going to ask Peko to just
share a little bit about that.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:30.200
Great.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:31.600
Thanks, Ian.

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:35.690
Yeah, I want to start by
echoing what you just said

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:39.347
and what Rick said,
which is that I think

00:26:39.347 --> 00:26:40.930
the faculty and
students have been put

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:42.730
in an extraordinary position.

00:26:42.730 --> 00:26:46.210
And the threat of the resilience
and the response that I've

00:26:46.210 --> 00:26:48.460
seen from both of
those communities

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:50.300
have been really remarkable.

00:26:50.300 --> 00:26:53.910
And that actually fills me with
a lot of optimism for the fall.

00:26:53.910 --> 00:26:58.600
To the students, I think some
of the faculty have adapted

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:00.370
and we're going to get better.

00:27:00.370 --> 00:27:03.700
It's kind of just get better
as we've learned more.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:07.750
So I think bigger, single sort
of umbrella thing that I wanted

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:09.435
to say is that I think MIT--

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:16.960
we've been sort of at our
best when we are stepping up

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:19.030
in the service of
the local community,

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.250
in the service of the
nation, and in the service

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:22.040
of the world.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:25.450
And I think that is not
just true in our research,

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:28.310
but also in our
educational efforts.

00:27:28.310 --> 00:27:30.460
So one of my colleagues,
Warren Seering,

00:27:30.460 --> 00:27:33.183
has a fabulous
presentation, where

00:27:33.183 --> 00:27:35.350
he shows the evolution of
the mechanical engineering

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:36.760
curriculum.

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:39.280
And you see that in
the late 1800s, when

00:27:39.280 --> 00:27:41.560
the nation was building
bridges, we were

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:43.563
teaching metallurgy of iron.

00:27:43.563 --> 00:27:44.980
Then we went on
the steam engines,

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:48.010
and we dropped metallurgy,
and we put in thermodynamics

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:49.660
and boiler design.

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:52.480
And then we went on
to electrification,

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:53.770
and we dropped boiler design.

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:56.062
And we went on to surveying
and electrical engineering.

00:27:56.062 --> 00:28:00.640
And so you can see that our
programs evolve according

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:04.870
to the National needs and
evolve to address problems that

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:06.910
are of immediate relevance.

00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:10.090
So I think we're going to
see more and more of that.

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:12.850
I think there are already a lot
of faculty that are doing that.

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:16.810
I think you're seeing groups of
that popping up around campus.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:20.560
And I applaud everybody
who's putting in that effort.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:21.060
Great.

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:21.780
Thanks, Peko.

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:26.430
So I think, as I said,
independent of what path we

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:30.210
choose in the fall-- and
I'm sort of optimistic that

00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:33.450
we'll have a path that has
at least some of our students

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:37.380
on campus for some of the
time, hopefully more--

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:40.170
I think there'll be some
really great experiences,

00:28:40.170 --> 00:28:44.430
great learning experiences
that befit MIT's history

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:47.160
and mission going forward.

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:50.040
And obviously, happy to take
lots of questions on that

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:50.970
later.

00:28:50.970 --> 00:28:52.230
Thanks, Rafael.

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:54.470
Thanks to you, Ian.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:57.510
Let me just say again,
as I said publicly,

00:28:57.510 --> 00:28:59.940
I thank you and admire you
for all the work you've done.

00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:02.280
It's almost [INAUDIBLE] to
bring it up at this point.

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:06.860
And Rick, thank you also for
joining us in this [INAUDIBLE]..

00:29:06.860 --> 00:29:11.700
You have no choice-- you have
to teach, but take you, anyway.

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:13.650
Let's move on to space.

00:29:13.650 --> 00:29:15.950
And we have associate
provost Krystyn Van

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:20.035
Vliet, who leads the
Working Group of Space Use.

00:29:20.035 --> 00:29:23.730
And Krystyn the question
that some people have is,

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.160
well, we have government
guidelines on safe distancing.

00:29:26.160 --> 00:29:29.670
How do you anticipate us
practicing those guidelines

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:30.810
in our classrooms?

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:36.430
Krystyn, are you
there somewhere?

00:29:36.430 --> 00:29:36.930
I am.

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:37.680
Thanks, Rafael.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.440
That's a very reasonable
question to ask.

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:42.600
Of course, it's on
all of our minds

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:45.570
as we bring even more
people onto the campus.

00:29:45.570 --> 00:29:48.180
As Suzy mentioned, we have
people who were on the campus

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:53.440
now but as we bring more people
back, how will we do this?

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.660
And I think this is a puzzle.

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:57.720
We enjoy solving
puzzles at MIT, and we

00:29:57.720 --> 00:29:59.850
have a very active
and dedicated group

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:01.560
who are thinking about this.

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:04.230
But what's already
clear is that there

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.780
will be a lower density of
people in any given space.

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:10.050
So if you think
about a lecture hall,

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:12.900
there will be fewer people
in that lecture hall,

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:16.830
but the education should
and will be just as vibrant.

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:19.710
But it's not just the lecture
halls which are obvious.

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:22.590
It's how well we do
performance-based learning

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:27.030
and project-based learning
and laboratory-based learning.

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:30.210
So we're thinking about
that from every curriculum's

00:30:30.210 --> 00:30:34.470
perspective, what will the
classroom of the future, when

00:30:34.470 --> 00:30:37.990
we bring more students
back to campus, look like?

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:42.540
And I think it's important to
be aware that that will always

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:43.930
have a few things in mind.

00:30:43.930 --> 00:30:46.650
It's not just about bringing
the students back to campus,

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:49.620
but it's about the
well-being of the students

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:51.690
and the faculty and the staff.

00:30:51.690 --> 00:30:54.340
And that's all
equally important.

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:56.550
And here, the staff
are the people

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.820
who helped to design and
deliver those classes,

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:03.870
to clean the restrooms and the
buildings and the laboratories.

00:31:03.870 --> 00:31:08.280
To take care of the research
teams and the administration.

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:11.370
And so as we think about
all of those people being

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:15.180
in the building together, we
think about the well-being

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:17.540
and the flexibility,
and the adaptability

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:21.078
as being important
components of the planning.

00:31:21.078 --> 00:31:21.870
Krystyn, thank you.

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:23.990
You've done, also,
an amazing job.

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:26.580
I think you're the only
or one of the very few

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:29.700
that is allowed to go into
every single space on campus.

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:30.970
We don't let anybody else.

00:31:30.970 --> 00:31:33.167
So that's a very
dubious privilege

00:31:33.167 --> 00:31:34.500
that you give yourself, Krystyn.

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:36.930
Thank you for the work you do.

00:31:36.930 --> 00:31:37.995
You're very welcome.

00:31:37.995 --> 00:31:39.870
And I should say, we're
all eager to get back

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:40.840
to the campus, Rafael.

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:43.158
The picture behind me I did
take a couple of weeks ago.

00:31:43.158 --> 00:31:44.700
So you're right, I
have the privilege

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:47.460
of getting to see the
campus in its current state,

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:49.620
but I'm just as eager
to be back in my lab

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:51.600
and my classroom as anyone else.

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:52.700
I agree with you, Krystyn.

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:53.880
Thank you.

00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:57.360
So let's move on to
the research engine.

00:31:57.360 --> 00:31:58.950
Lots of people are
asking where are

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:01.940
we going to bring the
research engine back.

00:32:01.940 --> 00:32:04.762
And Maria Zuber is
our VP for research.

00:32:04.762 --> 00:32:06.970
And hopefully, she is really
close with the question.

00:32:06.970 --> 00:32:08.280
Maria, please.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:09.780
Hi, everybody.

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:12.240
So our efforts in the
research enterprise

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:14.313
are in two critical areas.

00:32:14.313 --> 00:32:16.230
The first is funding
continuity and the second

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:20.160
is planning for a phased
opening of our research labs

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:23.310
in a way that prioritizes
the safety of both

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:25.920
the MIT and the
surrounding communities.

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:28.230
So in the area of
funding, we're closely

00:32:28.230 --> 00:32:30.930
monitoring our current
grants and future funding

00:32:30.930 --> 00:32:32.070
opportunities.

00:32:32.070 --> 00:32:35.340
Federal agencies
under OMB guidance

00:32:35.340 --> 00:32:39.480
have given great flexibility in
the execution of current grants

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:41.130
for a period of
90 days and we're

00:32:41.130 --> 00:32:42.660
working with
government colleagues

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:46.410
and our parents detentions to
advocate for not only funding

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:50.310
enhancements to existing
grants, but also,

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:53.830
talking about the importance
of proceeding with new proposal

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:55.560
solicitations.

00:32:55.560 --> 00:32:58.170
Our non-federal partners and
foundations and especially

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:01.110
in industry are undergoing
extraordinary financial

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:02.190
challenges.

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:04.500
And we're in communication
with many of them

00:33:04.500 --> 00:33:06.990
to try to minimize the
impacts to our researchers.

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:09.405
And we're working with our
vice president, the financier,

00:33:09.405 --> 00:33:13.320
and the provost to understand
the extent of our exposure

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:16.220
so we can develop
mitigating strategies.

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.690
Now to plan for the
ramp up of labs,

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:21.990
I instituted a
faculty-led process.

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:25.110
I hope virtual meetings
with over 20 department lab

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:27.810
and center heads to receive
input on the factors

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:30.660
that they considered important
in restarting research

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:32.100
on campus.

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:34.170
I then formed a
committee representing

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:38.460
a subset of this group
supplemented by critical staff

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:40.560
and chaired by
professor Tyler Jacks

00:33:40.560 --> 00:33:44.340
to develop guidelines for ramp
up, which we distributed widely

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:46.010
for comment.

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:49.040
The issues raised
were not all academic.

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:52.340
For example, among the
most often-cited needs

00:33:52.340 --> 00:33:55.490
are on campus parking
and child care.

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:57.680
The committee is now
coalescing the input

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:01.700
to develop an implementation
plan for a phased lab opening.

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:05.690
While we don't have a firm date
yet to announce at this time,

00:34:05.690 --> 00:34:08.210
we're following the guidelines
from the Massachusetts state

00:34:08.210 --> 00:34:10.045
officials very closely.

00:34:10.045 --> 00:34:11.420
And I'm now going
to turn it over

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:15.860
to Tyler to provide an update
on the committee's activities.

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:18.710
Well, thanks very much, Maria,
and good afternoon, everybody.

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:22.070
Let me say at the outset that
this was a very engaged process

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:26.690
that involved nine MIT faculty
members and several senior MIT

00:34:26.690 --> 00:34:29.120
staff, who worked
really effectively

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:30.679
to come up with a
set of guidelines

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:33.260
to help us think
about a sensible way

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:36.020
to come back to our
research activities.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:39.330
I'll talk about those in
some detail in a second,

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:42.960
but let me start by making
a few general points.

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:45.679
The first is that although
most of our research activities

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:48.230
have moved to the
off campus setting

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:49.909
over the last couple
of months, there

00:34:49.909 --> 00:34:52.760
is significant research
taking place at MIT,

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:56.480
including quite a lot in the
area of COVID-19 research.

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:59.330
And Bruce Walker actually
mentioned some of that earlier.

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:01.730
And that's quite exciting.

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:04.560
Secondly, when Maria
constituted the committee,

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:07.130
she was very clear
about the scope,

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:11.600
and specifically that it related
to research that needed access

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:14.660
to laboratories or needed
access to equipment in order

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:15.860
to be done.

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:19.190
And therefore, we assume
that the current policy that

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:21.470
work that can be
done remotely will

00:35:21.470 --> 00:35:25.100
be done remotely we'll stay
in place during this phase

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:27.050
of the research ramp up.

00:35:27.050 --> 00:35:32.150
And the third general point is
that we recognize that when we

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:34.340
come back to campus
in this fashion,

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:38.750
there will be individuals who
cannot or choose not to come

00:35:38.750 --> 00:35:41.490
back for a variety of reasons.

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:43.010
And so the committee
was very clear

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:47.030
that with respect to all members
of research teams coming back,

00:35:47.030 --> 00:35:49.390
it would be voluntary.

00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:51.100
With respect to the
planning itself,

00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:53.470
the committee took
the approach that we

00:35:53.470 --> 00:35:55.860
wanted to get back to research.

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:58.390
We're all anxious to
get back to research,

00:35:58.390 --> 00:36:00.280
but we have to do
it in a fashion that

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:03.850
represents the safest
possible set of practices.

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:07.810
So therefore, we anticipate
starting with a low density

00:36:07.810 --> 00:36:10.600
work environment as
Krystyn described,

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:13.540
that will allow us to be
well within CDC guidelines

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:16.090
in terms of safe work practices.

00:36:16.090 --> 00:36:18.640
And over time as
conditions allow,

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:22.300
we envision future phases
that allow more individuals

00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:24.940
on campus at a given time.

00:36:24.940 --> 00:36:28.630
To stay within the prescribed
density and occupancy

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:33.340
parameters, groups will have to
develop shift work schedules,

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:38.350
either multiple shifts per day
or alternative day schedules.

00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:40.870
The committee left the
development of these plans

00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:43.120
to each individual
faculty member

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:49.600
or principal investigator with
oversight from their DLC heads.

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:52.210
We've also discussed
additional safety measures that

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:55.000
have been already
described in this meeting,

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.040
and these will
include mandated use

00:36:57.040 --> 00:37:00.910
of personal protective
equipment, daily health checks,

00:37:00.910 --> 00:37:04.480
as well as frequent sanitization
of personal workspaces

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:06.450
in common areas.

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:09.210
Lastly, the committee
was clear about the need

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:12.390
during the ramp up phase to
integrate the level of research

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:16.140
activity across campus so as
to ensure that shared research

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:19.800
facilities as well as
MIT central services

00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:21.630
are not overburdened.

00:37:21.630 --> 00:37:24.930
And that the staff who are
performing these functions also

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:26.850
have safe work conditions.

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:29.870
So I'll stop there and
turn it back to you, Maria.

00:37:29.870 --> 00:37:32.040
OK, and obviously, in
all of this planning,

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:36.342
we've been assisted by
our Medical professionals

00:37:36.342 --> 00:37:37.800
and also we've
gotten a lot of help

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:41.170
from Krystyn's office in
space and many others.

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:43.650
So Rafael, we'll
turn it back to you.

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:45.690
Thank you, Maria and
thank you, Tyler.

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:47.940
Look, I mean, needless to
say, the research enterprise

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:50.310
is so important to all of us.

00:37:50.310 --> 00:37:52.350
I'm grateful that the
people in the piece

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:54.600
are the two of you are willing
to just give us so much

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:56.975
of your time to make sure that
we bring that engine back.

00:37:56.975 --> 00:37:58.260
Much appreciated.

00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:05.850
Let's move quickly to
community and related issues.

00:38:05.850 --> 00:38:09.000
I think we're ready
to go to the Q&amp;A,

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:12.570
but I would like us to stop
on our cultural community.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:15.120
And for that, we
have Ramona Allen,

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:17.340
our VP for human
resources, and we

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:20.010
have Jill Dozier, our
community and equity officer.

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:21.760
Let's start with Ramona.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:26.640
Ramona, we have about 10,000
people at MIT working.

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:30.330
So whatever we do thinking
about the faculty and students,

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:32.400
clearly, we have to think
about the staff, too.

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:34.920
They are a significant
fraction of MIT on campus.

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:39.470
So what do you know about
how is the staff doing?

00:38:39.470 --> 00:38:41.710
Do they enjoy working from home?

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:44.490
How are they holding out?

00:38:44.490 --> 00:38:48.510
I think staff are coping
pretty well, actually.

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:51.210
I think they've done
extraordinary things

00:38:51.210 --> 00:38:52.800
over the past several weeks.

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:54.420
I guess it's eight weeks now.

00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:57.870
It's hard to believe that
that much time had gone by.

00:38:57.870 --> 00:38:59.740
I think they're
doing extremely well.

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:02.310
But there are also
some concerns.

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:05.270
Staff want to come back to
campus just like everybody

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:05.770
else.

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:08.730
They're interested in helping
get our community back up

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:09.990
and running.

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:13.080
And they want to support all
of the efforts of the faculty

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:14.510
and the students, as well.

00:39:14.510 --> 00:39:16.007
And we miss each other.

00:39:16.007 --> 00:39:17.340
We want to be around each other.

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:18.390
We're social beings.

00:39:18.390 --> 00:39:21.600
We need to be together.

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:25.090
I think when you look
at all these scenarios,

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:28.210
we have to think about how this
is going to apply to staff.

00:39:28.210 --> 00:39:32.600
And I think first and foremost,
when people return to campus--

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:36.570
and it'll probably
be in a phased way--

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:39.480
staff need to
understand those of us,

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:41.190
especially in
administrative roles,

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:42.870
that we might not
be the first people

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:45.270
to be asked to come
back to campus.

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:48.120
I'm in that category.

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:51.720
When I look at administrative
people across the campus,

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:55.800
that's many of us who might
not be in the first round

00:39:55.800 --> 00:39:58.150
of the reentry process.

00:39:58.150 --> 00:40:00.810
And so I think people have
to be patient with that

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:02.610
and be understanding,
because when

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:05.730
you bring staff
back to the campus,

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:08.670
that means more deliveries.

00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:11.200
Other staff will be
needed to be on campus.

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:15.030
We're going to need more
facility staff, more police,

00:40:15.030 --> 00:40:16.920
more security, you name it.

00:40:16.920 --> 00:40:19.290
So that just increases
things overall

00:40:19.290 --> 00:40:22.600
for every single category.

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:25.690
And we also have to look at
how people get to campus.

00:40:25.690 --> 00:40:30.058
So if people are taking public
transportation, for example,

00:40:30.058 --> 00:40:32.100
you're going to have more
people on these trains.

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:34.710
Now it's my understanding that
there's some social distancing

00:40:34.710 --> 00:40:39.840
measures that are being
implemented on subway systems

00:40:39.840 --> 00:40:43.320
so that people will feel safe
when they're coming to work,

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:46.140
because sometimes, that's
the only way many of us

00:40:46.140 --> 00:40:47.247
can get to work.

00:40:47.247 --> 00:40:48.330
So those are a few things.

00:40:48.330 --> 00:40:52.643
So there are people who will
need to have back on campus

00:40:52.643 --> 00:40:54.060
pretty quickly,
and then there are

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:57.690
those who may have to wait
before we are allowed back

00:40:57.690 --> 00:40:58.530
on campus.

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:01.500
And it's basically
for our own safety.

00:41:01.500 --> 00:41:04.480
And we have to just
be patient about that.

00:41:04.480 --> 00:41:07.920
There are masks.

00:41:07.920 --> 00:41:10.500
Cambridge is requiring
everybody to wear masks.

00:41:10.500 --> 00:41:12.840
Do we provide those masks?

00:41:12.840 --> 00:41:14.520
Do we ask visitors to wear them?

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:15.750
How do we enforce it?

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:18.900
Lots of things that
are in play here.

00:41:18.900 --> 00:41:22.050
Lots of people around campus who
are thinking about this deeply

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:26.490
who care about It And we've been
spending a lot of time on it.

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:29.340
So I think in general, staff
are handling things very well.

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:31.710
I think they are
eager to get back

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:34.135
to campus just like
everybody else.

00:41:34.135 --> 00:41:35.405
So thank you.

00:41:35.405 --> 00:41:36.380
Ramona, thank you.

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:38.040
Thank you for what
you do with HR.

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:40.060
I really appreciate it.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:44.962
John, you have been at
MIT for how long already?

00:41:44.962 --> 00:41:46.740
All of 49 days.

00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:51.040
Wow, you almost have
a degree of MIT.

00:41:51.040 --> 00:41:53.160
John, I hear that
you're organizing

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:55.900
a service for all staff,
students, and faculty.

00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:57.720
So can you tell us a
little more about it?

00:41:57.720 --> 00:41:58.800
Sure thing.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:00.630
Thank you, Rafael.

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:04.080
One of the characteristics that
makes MIT special in many ways,

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:07.980
both formal and informal,
that we can connect and engage

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:10.020
with others in our community.

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:12.960
The sense of connection,
belonging, and inclusion

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:15.820
is vital not only to the
strength of the community,

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:18.480
but also to the health and
well-being of people in it.

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:21.900
We therefore must
ensure that we continue

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:23.280
benefiting to the
greatest extent

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:26.590
possible from the diversity
of people, perspectives,

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:28.920
experiences, and
engagement opportunities

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:31.530
that enrich our individual
and collective teaching,

00:42:31.530 --> 00:42:33.610
learning, research,
scholarship, work,

00:42:33.610 --> 00:42:35.340
and decision-making--
while doing

00:42:35.340 --> 00:42:40.050
so on what is for most of us, a
completely online environment.

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:43.260
I'm working with Tim Jamison
and Maryanne Kirkbride

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.480
to lead a community
continuity group that's

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.340
comprised of a
representative team, who

00:42:47.340 --> 00:42:50.160
will guide both
short term efforts

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:51.900
and a longer term
strategy that's

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:53.400
focused on these concerns.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:56.010
We initially responded
to a rising community

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:58.350
need for essential
information and assembled

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:01.380
most of our online
community support resources

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:02.770
in one place--

00:43:02.770 --> 00:43:05.430
on the ICO website with a
link to this information

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:07.500
on the COVID-19 website.

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:09.517
Rafael, regarding
how we will gain

00:43:09.517 --> 00:43:11.100
and understanding
how our community is

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:13.560
doing specifically,
we understand

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:16.050
that there are many
leads that exist

00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:18.480
amongst the members of our
community since the beginning

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:20.340
of our response to
the COVID crisis.

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:23.670
But today, we have little
empirical evidence of the scope

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:25.890
and scale of these needs.

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:28.410
As such, we're tapping
into our faculty expertise

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:30.960
and working with
institutional research

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:33.630
to administer a series
of poll surveys--

00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:37.050
short surveys that take a
few minutes to complete that

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:38.550
will help us to
better understand

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:42.240
where to target our attention
and resources, thereby

00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:44.940
maximizing the sense of
community, connectedness,

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:48.973
and belonging for all students,
postdocs, staff, and faculty.

00:43:48.973 --> 00:43:50.640
This is one of the
many tools that we'll

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:54.060
be using to stay connected,
understand, and respond

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:57.630
to our community's balding
needs in an ongoing fashion

00:43:57.630 --> 00:43:59.820
during the uncertain
times ahead of us.

00:43:59.820 --> 00:44:01.850
Our plan is to launch
the first survey

00:44:01.850 --> 00:44:03.422
before the end of this month.

00:44:03.422 --> 00:44:05.130
And we will, of course,
share the results

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:06.840
with the MIT community.

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:09.330
If anyone has questions
or recommendations

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:11.190
for the community
continuity group,

00:44:11.190 --> 00:44:15.840
I ask that you email us at
communitycontinuity@mit.edu.

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:17.320
Thanks, Rafael.

00:44:17.320 --> 00:44:18.450
John, thank you so much.

00:44:18.450 --> 00:44:19.770
Thank you for joining us.

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:23.130
And I bet you those 49 days
and not being boring at all.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:25.210
They haven't.

00:44:25.210 --> 00:44:25.950
Thank you, John.

00:44:25.950 --> 00:44:31.950
So let's not move to the Q&amp;A
part of the conversation.

00:44:31.950 --> 00:44:35.400
Let me just say that for this
part of the conversation,

00:44:35.400 --> 00:44:38.060
we have also joining
us to answer questions,

00:44:38.060 --> 00:44:40.320
our provost, Marty Schmidt,
our chancellor, Cindy

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:44.080
Barnhart, our CP for Open
Learning, Sanjay Sarma,

00:44:44.080 --> 00:44:47.070
and our VP and general
counsel, Mark DiVincenzo.

00:44:47.070 --> 00:44:50.240
So Alfred, do you have any
questions rising to the top

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:51.600
that you want us to answer?

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:52.350
Yes, indeed.

00:44:52.350 --> 00:44:54.510
We have a whole bunch of
questions-- nearly 200

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:56.640
at this point, and
they've all been terrific.

00:44:56.640 --> 00:45:00.690
So just a quick reminder from
me as we move into the Q&amp;A--

00:45:00.690 --> 00:45:03.150
reminder, you can still submit
questions using the link

00:45:03.150 --> 00:45:05.050
near the bottom of your screen.

00:45:05.050 --> 00:45:06.450
We're not going
to try and answer

00:45:06.450 --> 00:45:08.280
in the order in
which these came in.

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:11.718
Instead, we're focusing on
the most frequent questions.

00:45:11.718 --> 00:45:13.260
And just a reminder,
of course, there

00:45:13.260 --> 00:45:15.900
are some questions we
simply don't have answers

00:45:15.900 --> 00:45:17.460
for just yet, but
we'll let you know

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:19.030
what we know at this stage.

00:45:19.030 --> 00:45:22.830
And so Rafael, by my
reckoning here, certainly,

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:25.260
the number one
questions have to do

00:45:25.260 --> 00:45:30.420
with budget and financial
support for students.

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:33.420
I think the most frequently
asked questions so far has

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:35.970
to do with tuition in the fall.

00:45:35.970 --> 00:45:40.080
If it's going to be a fall
semester primarily online,

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:43.110
will that affect tuition,
that's one question.

00:45:43.110 --> 00:45:47.370
And a related one has
to do with our support

00:45:47.370 --> 00:45:48.930
for graduate students.

00:45:48.930 --> 00:45:51.930
We know that there has
been a petition circulating

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:55.380
to ensure that grad students
get the support they need,

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:59.580
and that's a question that's
been coming up, as well.

00:45:59.580 --> 00:46:01.940
Let's see with Marty,
our management provost.

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:04.695
Marty, can you answer
those questions. one

00:46:04.695 --> 00:46:06.450
is tuition in the fall.

00:46:06.450 --> 00:46:08.880
I think the way I
understood you now--

00:46:08.880 --> 00:46:11.700
if it goes online, what
will that do to tuition,

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:15.250
and support for grad
students and the petition.

00:46:15.250 --> 00:46:16.970
Marty, are you on?

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:18.270
Yeah, I am, Rafael.

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:18.966
Can you hear me?

00:46:18.966 --> 00:46:19.466
Perfect.

00:46:19.466 --> 00:46:20.320
Yes, we can.

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:24.800
Become accustomed to
turning off the mute.

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:27.740
So yeah, let me try and
answer that, Rafael.

00:46:27.740 --> 00:46:29.540
As you know, under
our normal process,

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:31.850
each year, we announce
tuition and financial aid

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:33.530
in their early
spring, and that's

00:46:33.530 --> 00:46:36.110
exactly what we did
this year, where

00:46:36.110 --> 00:46:38.360
we announced tuition
and fees for next year

00:46:38.360 --> 00:46:41.150
would increase by 3.8%.

00:46:41.150 --> 00:46:42.620
But we also
announced an increase

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:46.040
in the undergraduate
financial aid budget to 7.8%.

00:46:46.040 --> 00:46:48.770
And just as a footnote, our
undergraduate financial aid

00:46:48.770 --> 00:46:50.960
budget for the
coming fiscal year,

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:54.350
we budgeted at $148 million.

00:46:54.350 --> 00:46:56.540
But clearly, these are
no longer normal times.

00:46:56.540 --> 00:46:59.320
And we're focused on trying
to determine what the fall

00:46:59.320 --> 00:47:01.167
and spring will look like.

00:47:01.167 --> 00:47:02.750
Without that picture,
it's really hard

00:47:02.750 --> 00:47:05.670
to think about tuition in the
normal way which we do it.

00:47:05.670 --> 00:47:09.350
So our focus right now is what
will fall and spring look like.

00:47:09.350 --> 00:47:11.240
And then we'll
make determinations

00:47:11.240 --> 00:47:16.580
about the financial needs for
the students and the tuition.

00:47:16.580 --> 00:47:18.500
I will say that in
our re budgeting

00:47:18.500 --> 00:47:22.070
process, as we experienced
in the 2009 crisis,

00:47:22.070 --> 00:47:25.160
we fully anticipate that the
students' need for support

00:47:25.160 --> 00:47:27.470
will go up as you
know don't have

00:47:27.470 --> 00:47:29.600
to look any further than
the 30 million people that

00:47:29.600 --> 00:47:32.340
have filed for unemployment.

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:34.820
And so for that purpose, we
have actually increased even

00:47:34.820 --> 00:47:38.090
beyond the $147 million,
our budgeted financial aid

00:47:38.090 --> 00:47:40.217
budget for next year.

00:47:40.217 --> 00:47:42.050
The last thing I think
it's important for me

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:44.210
to point out when we're
thinking about tuition--

00:47:44.210 --> 00:47:47.690
it's important to recognize
that 60% of our undergraduates

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:50.180
today receive MIT scholarships.

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:52.400
And actually, 31% of
the undergraduates

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:57.320
don't pay tuition based
upon important need.

00:47:57.320 --> 00:47:59.120
So that's the
question on tuition.

00:47:59.120 --> 00:48:01.730
I think you also
asked about what we're

00:48:01.730 --> 00:48:03.890
doing for graduate students.

00:48:03.890 --> 00:48:06.140
And this is an area that
we've been working really

00:48:06.140 --> 00:48:08.360
hard on with both
the deans, the vice

00:48:08.360 --> 00:48:10.880
chancellor, and the
chancellor, Cindy Barnhart.

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:12.410
I'll say a few
things and then Cindy

00:48:12.410 --> 00:48:16.790
may want to say more maybe
just to correct things

00:48:16.790 --> 00:48:18.920
that I don't get it right.

00:48:18.920 --> 00:48:21.510
We're taking this
issue in several steps.

00:48:21.510 --> 00:48:23.510
The first step is really
to address the concerns

00:48:23.510 --> 00:48:25.070
about summer support.

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:27.350
So last week, we announced
the establishment

00:48:27.350 --> 00:48:29.270
of a short term
emergency fund that's

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:32.600
going to address instances where
students' summer plans have

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:35.300
been disrupted and
they need support.

00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:38.810
There's a lot of diversity in
the amount and type of need

00:48:38.810 --> 00:48:39.650
that's needed.

00:48:39.650 --> 00:48:42.350
And this is really based
on the vast variety

00:48:42.350 --> 00:48:43.840
of graduate programs
that we have.

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:46.550
And for that reason, we're
working with each school

00:48:46.550 --> 00:48:48.440
and college dean
to ensure that we

00:48:48.440 --> 00:48:50.840
have the resources to
address the full need

00:48:50.840 --> 00:48:52.640
for their particular programs.

00:48:52.640 --> 00:48:54.890
And that those resources are
allocated in a way that's

00:48:54.890 --> 00:48:56.810
appropriate for the local need.

00:48:56.810 --> 00:49:00.080
I'm confident that we can meet
the full need of those schools,

00:49:00.080 --> 00:49:03.200
but we have to address it
at that local level in terms

00:49:03.200 --> 00:49:05.060
of assessing the need.

00:49:05.060 --> 00:49:07.250
I've met as recently
as today with the deans

00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:10.530
to confirm our plans
and review them.

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:13.000
The second step is
really to address impacts

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:15.380
the COVID-19 is having
on students' progress

00:49:15.380 --> 00:49:17.793
towards their degree and the
legitimate concerns about

00:49:17.793 --> 00:49:20.210
whether or not they're going
to be supported into the fall

00:49:20.210 --> 00:49:24.148
term, what that means about
their support, degrees,

00:49:24.148 --> 00:49:25.190
extensions, and the like.

00:49:25.190 --> 00:49:27.830
And that's where we'll
turn our attention next

00:49:27.830 --> 00:49:30.380
once we have the summer
support issue nailed, which

00:49:30.380 --> 00:49:33.268
is obviously the most pressing.

00:49:33.268 --> 00:49:35.060
I'd also say that I'm
really pleased to say

00:49:35.060 --> 00:49:37.010
that last week we
announced that we accepted

00:49:37.010 --> 00:49:39.920
the recommendation developed by
the Graduate Student Council's

00:49:39.920 --> 00:49:41.660
Graduate Stipend
Committee, where

00:49:41.660 --> 00:49:45.740
we'll be increasing graduate
students' stipends by 2.9%.

00:49:45.740 --> 00:49:47.690
And we've also announced
that health insurance

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:49.730
rates for our Medical
plans will not

00:49:49.730 --> 00:49:51.880
increase in the coming year.

00:49:51.880 --> 00:49:53.630
So those are the things
I wanted to share.

00:49:53.630 --> 00:49:55.713
I don't know-- Cindy, you
want to add more or back

00:49:55.713 --> 00:49:58.120
to you, Rafael?

00:49:58.120 --> 00:49:59.782
Good job, Marty.

00:49:59.782 --> 00:50:00.610
Right.

00:50:00.610 --> 00:50:01.720
Thank you, Martin.

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:03.990
Alfred, do you have another one?

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:05.320
I sure do.

00:50:05.320 --> 00:50:07.390
In that same vein,
a number of people

00:50:07.390 --> 00:50:11.320
are asking about our support
for international students, who

00:50:11.320 --> 00:50:14.290
face other kinds of hurdles
some of them financial,

00:50:14.290 --> 00:50:18.550
but of course, questions of
travel bans and visa issues.

00:50:18.550 --> 00:50:20.530
So a number of
questions are asking

00:50:20.530 --> 00:50:26.090
how MIT plans to support
our international students.

00:50:26.090 --> 00:50:28.130
Very good question.

00:50:28.130 --> 00:50:29.950
Maria Zuber, our
VP for research,

00:50:29.950 --> 00:50:32.490
you want to take a shot at it?

00:50:32.490 --> 00:50:35.870
Or maybe I'll ask Cindy--

00:50:35.870 --> 00:50:36.790
it's OK, Maria?

00:50:36.790 --> 00:50:41.140
OK, so I'll start, and then
Cindy may want to jump in.

00:50:41.140 --> 00:50:45.440
So we're monitoring
the situation closely.

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:48.430
Of course, we continue
to be in contact

00:50:48.430 --> 00:50:51.940
with our colleagues
in Washington

00:50:51.940 --> 00:50:56.170
to try to explain the
importance of getting

00:50:56.170 --> 00:51:00.160
these international
scholars onto our campuses.

00:51:00.160 --> 00:51:04.870
We're monitoring the
situation closely

00:51:04.870 --> 00:51:09.100
and we're in touch
with these students

00:51:09.100 --> 00:51:13.880
to try to essentially be
ready to hit Send as soon

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:19.000
as the visa situation
opens up to get

00:51:19.000 --> 00:51:22.030
everything submitted on time.

00:51:22.030 --> 00:51:24.520
Cindy, if you want to
add anything to that.

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:28.840
I think maybe Mark may
want to comment on this.

00:51:28.840 --> 00:51:30.160
Mark?

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:30.660
Sure.

00:51:30.660 --> 00:51:36.330
So we understand that there
are a number of circumstances

00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:39.390
beyond the usual circumstances
of international students

00:51:39.390 --> 00:51:41.970
and scholars being able
to freely come to MIT

00:51:41.970 --> 00:51:43.810
and to stay at MIT.

00:51:43.810 --> 00:51:47.670
And so we're looking at a
number of different scenarios

00:51:47.670 --> 00:51:51.990
in which we understand
that potentially, there

00:51:51.990 --> 00:51:55.380
might be remote research,
remote learning.

00:51:55.380 --> 00:51:58.170
And we have a small
group right now looking

00:51:58.170 --> 00:52:02.520
at all those situations to
see what the options are,

00:52:02.520 --> 00:52:07.620
what our flexibility is, what
the legal constraints may be,

00:52:07.620 --> 00:52:10.050
what negative effects
some of our practices

00:52:10.050 --> 00:52:12.120
might have on people's
visa statuses,

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:15.330
if they're now in their home
country and may not return.

00:52:15.330 --> 00:52:17.010
All with the
understanding that we

00:52:17.010 --> 00:52:21.900
want to support the continuing
scholarship in education

00:52:21.900 --> 00:52:26.460
over international students
as well as the new community

00:52:26.460 --> 00:52:28.410
members that are coming to MIT.

00:52:28.410 --> 00:52:30.150
So all I can say is
we're working hard.

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:32.565
We're trying to have
flexible options.

00:52:32.565 --> 00:52:34.440
We're working with the
international students

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:36.270
office, the international
scholars office,

00:52:36.270 --> 00:52:39.540
as well as our vice president
of finance office, and my office

00:52:39.540 --> 00:52:43.140
to come up with a scenario to
understand both the compliance

00:52:43.140 --> 00:52:46.230
issues as well as to
ensure that we're providing

00:52:46.230 --> 00:52:48.960
as much flexible options for
our international students

00:52:48.960 --> 00:52:50.810
and scholars.

00:52:50.810 --> 00:52:52.770
Thank you, Mark.

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:55.230
Just I forgo to ask
Maria a question.

00:52:55.230 --> 00:52:56.490
Maria, can you say something?

00:52:56.490 --> 00:52:59.190
I want to see you on the
screen one more time.

00:52:59.190 --> 00:53:00.490
OK, I'm back.

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:02.220
At the first
townhall, I asked you

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:04.807
what was your screen
background and I

00:53:04.807 --> 00:53:06.390
have to ask the
question to you again.

00:53:06.390 --> 00:53:08.490
What is the screen
background right now?

00:53:08.490 --> 00:53:09.010
OK.

00:53:09.010 --> 00:53:11.302
The last time I was on
Mars, and I'm still on Mars,

00:53:11.302 --> 00:53:12.510
but I'm in a different place.

00:53:12.510 --> 00:53:15.610
I'm actually in the western
part of the Valles Marineris,

00:53:15.610 --> 00:53:20.060
which is a canyon that's about
as wide as the United States.

00:53:20.060 --> 00:53:21.952
Well, I assume that
was our right answer,

00:53:21.952 --> 00:53:22.910
because I have no idea.

00:53:22.910 --> 00:53:25.260
But thank you, Maria.

00:53:25.260 --> 00:53:26.880
Alfred, you got another one?

00:53:26.880 --> 00:53:27.480
Sure do.

00:53:27.480 --> 00:53:31.950
And this, again, is coming
from a number of folks.

00:53:31.950 --> 00:53:35.880
So let me start here-- actually,
maybe Bruce will help us

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:36.810
with these questions.

00:53:36.810 --> 00:53:41.260
It goes back to how will
we ensure safety on campus,

00:53:41.260 --> 00:53:46.650
Rafael, when and if students
are returning in the fall.

00:53:46.650 --> 00:53:48.510
A few questions related to that.

00:53:48.510 --> 00:53:52.140
A lot are asking how will we
ensure the safety of students

00:53:52.140 --> 00:53:55.110
and staff on campus.

00:53:55.110 --> 00:53:59.430
A related question is,
will MIT supply facemasks?

00:53:59.430 --> 00:54:05.230
And the third in this group
is, do facemasks do the trick,

00:54:05.230 --> 00:54:10.110
and do we need social distancing
if we're all wearing masks?

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:12.810
Well, clearly, I
think that Bruce

00:54:12.810 --> 00:54:14.550
is a little more
qualified than I

00:54:14.550 --> 00:54:18.420
am to answer the question of
face masks or what we need.

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:23.440
I think what I can say is that
we will consider seriously

00:54:23.440 --> 00:54:25.500
about the supply of
face masks and how

00:54:25.500 --> 00:54:28.150
to make things easy for
everybody in the community.

00:54:28.150 --> 00:54:30.210
That is where we're going.

00:54:30.210 --> 00:54:32.550
But in terms of are we
going to be asking everybody

00:54:32.550 --> 00:54:35.020
to wear masks, if a
decision is made today

00:54:35.020 --> 00:54:38.640
with what we know today, unless
Bruce tells me that I'm wrong,

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:39.600
the answer is yes.

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:42.690
Will we know anything in
four weeks or eight weeks

00:54:42.690 --> 00:54:45.910
or in four months to
decide differently?

00:54:45.910 --> 00:54:46.770
Perhaps.

00:54:46.770 --> 00:54:48.930
I'll leave that crystal
ball up to Bruce.

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:53.310
But I think as you heard
Bruce's comments earlier,

00:54:53.310 --> 00:54:57.570
a face mask is a safe way to
proceed with the knowledge we

00:54:57.570 --> 00:54:58.800
have as of today.

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:00.385
And we're running
along those lines.

00:55:00.385 --> 00:55:02.760
But Bruce, do you want to add
anything to what I've said?

00:55:08.150 --> 00:55:11.550
OK, I guess [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:11.550 --> 00:55:13.550
Just to agree that
I think face masks

00:55:13.550 --> 00:55:16.770
are really a critical
component here.

00:55:16.770 --> 00:55:21.420
And I think there's every reason
to believe that coming back

00:55:21.420 --> 00:55:25.740
to campus is going to be
associated with everybody

00:55:25.740 --> 00:55:26.850
wearing face masks.

00:55:26.850 --> 00:55:29.580
It's hard for me to believe
that by September that will not

00:55:29.580 --> 00:55:32.220
still be a recommendation.

00:55:32.220 --> 00:55:33.160
Thank you, Bruce.

00:55:33.160 --> 00:55:35.440
And Rafael, may I--

00:55:35.440 --> 00:55:35.940
go ahead.

00:55:35.940 --> 00:55:36.940
Someone else will speak.

00:55:36.940 --> 00:55:39.750
I think that there was a sort
of related question about if one

00:55:39.750 --> 00:55:43.300
wears a face mask, does one not
need to do social distancing?

00:55:43.300 --> 00:55:46.530
And I think that the simple
answer is everything helps.

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:51.540
So most likely, face masks
will be part of the process.

00:55:51.540 --> 00:55:56.610
And one will be advised to
practice social distancing

00:55:56.610 --> 00:55:59.700
policies for the
foreseeable future.

00:55:59.700 --> 00:56:00.420
Thank you, Tyler.

00:56:00.420 --> 00:56:01.170
I missed that one.

00:56:01.170 --> 00:56:01.990
I appreciate it.

00:56:01.990 --> 00:56:03.810
Thank you.

00:56:03.810 --> 00:56:05.770
Alfred, you have another one?

00:56:05.770 --> 00:56:08.160
Yes, and this is another
very, very popular question,

00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:12.120
having to do with when we think
we'll be able to make decisions

00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:15.170
about the fall, Rafael.

00:56:15.170 --> 00:56:21.790
Well, let me just say that this
is a pretty serious question

00:56:21.790 --> 00:56:27.520
that we are thinking about on
a daily basis, pretty much.

00:56:27.520 --> 00:56:29.240
With what I know today--

00:56:29.240 --> 00:56:32.540
and again, I rely heavily with
advice from people like Bruce

00:56:32.540 --> 00:56:34.730
and Taylor earlier,
I mean, people

00:56:34.730 --> 00:56:37.970
who understand this
much more than I do,

00:56:37.970 --> 00:56:42.180
I would like to wait
for as long as possible.

00:56:42.180 --> 00:56:44.780
But we know things
might still change.

00:56:44.780 --> 00:56:49.200
I doubt that even if
these are still changing,

00:56:49.200 --> 00:56:51.223
we'll wait much longer
than eight weeks

00:56:51.223 --> 00:56:52.140
to make that decision.

00:56:52.140 --> 00:56:56.210
So I would say that late
June, early July, thereabouts,

00:56:56.210 --> 00:57:00.320
we should know enough to try
to make the right decision

00:57:00.320 --> 00:57:01.940
for the campus.

00:57:01.940 --> 00:57:04.820
But as you heard
from Bruce earlier,

00:57:04.820 --> 00:57:06.405
things are changing
very rapidly,

00:57:06.405 --> 00:57:08.030
and we're monitoring
that very rapidly.

00:57:08.030 --> 00:57:10.910
If at some point in time
between now and then

00:57:10.910 --> 00:57:13.153
we understand that
things will change much,

00:57:13.153 --> 00:57:14.570
we'll be ready to
make a decision.

00:57:14.570 --> 00:57:16.460
But while things are
changing and we're

00:57:16.460 --> 00:57:18.880
learning more and
more every day,

00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:20.840
I think the right
and responsible thing

00:57:20.840 --> 00:57:23.640
to do is to wait until
the last possible moment,

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:27.200
although not much later
than early July or so on

00:57:27.200 --> 00:57:28.290
to make that decision.

00:57:28.290 --> 00:57:30.230
That's where we
are at this point.

00:57:30.230 --> 00:57:35.840
Rafael, I might add that I think
that the fact of the matter

00:57:35.840 --> 00:57:39.100
is that at the Brigham
and Women's Hospital--

00:57:39.100 --> 00:57:41.390
I learned this morning--

00:57:41.390 --> 00:57:45.560
despite all the COVID patients
there, despite all the activity

00:57:45.560 --> 00:57:49.970
and the chaos that's accompanied
this surge in Boston,

00:57:49.970 --> 00:57:55.070
there have been only two
cases of transmission

00:57:55.070 --> 00:57:58.400
to health care workers
that were documented

00:57:58.400 --> 00:58:00.470
that could be attributed
to something happening

00:58:00.470 --> 00:58:02.610
within the hospital.

00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:05.930
Obviously, MIT is not the same
as being inside the Brigham,

00:58:05.930 --> 00:58:09.860
but it really points to
the fact that judicious use

00:58:09.860 --> 00:58:14.150
of face masks is something that
really does make a difference.

00:58:14.150 --> 00:58:16.940
Social distancing is not as
possible in the hospitals,

00:58:16.940 --> 00:58:18.980
so it's probably not
adhered to as much.

00:58:18.980 --> 00:58:22.490
But to Tyler's point,
social distancing

00:58:22.490 --> 00:58:26.300
will also have to
be something that's

00:58:26.300 --> 00:58:28.930
implemented and maintained.

00:58:28.930 --> 00:58:31.830
Thank you, Bruce.

00:58:31.830 --> 00:58:33.700
Alfred, you have
any other questions?

00:58:33.700 --> 00:58:34.720
I sure do.

00:58:34.720 --> 00:58:37.830
And this one, Rafael, is maybe
more specifically for you

00:58:37.830 --> 00:58:39.537
and maybe Ramona.

00:58:39.537 --> 00:58:42.120
President Reif, you wrote last
month that you'll do everything

00:58:42.120 --> 00:58:43.770
you can to avoid cutting jobs.

00:58:43.770 --> 00:58:47.440
Is that still the case?

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:49.428
Well, let me see
how Ramona answers,

00:58:49.428 --> 00:58:50.970
and I'll try to
correct what she say.

00:58:50.970 --> 00:58:52.670
Ramona, you want
to give it a shot?

00:58:52.670 --> 00:58:54.250
Absolutely.

00:58:54.250 --> 00:58:58.690
So layoffs are a
last resort, always.

00:58:58.690 --> 00:59:01.120
None of us wants to go
through that activity.

00:59:01.120 --> 00:59:04.150
We understand the
economic climate.

00:59:04.150 --> 00:59:07.750
We know that if you were to
leave MIT to loss of job,

00:59:07.750 --> 00:59:10.900
you're going out into a
really tough situation.

00:59:10.900 --> 00:59:14.540
We are trying to avoid
that at all costs.

00:59:14.540 --> 00:59:19.450
So we're doing everything we can
to think this through and make

00:59:19.450 --> 00:59:22.180
sure a number of
people weigh in.

00:59:22.180 --> 00:59:24.950
And those decisions are
taking very seriously.

00:59:24.950 --> 00:59:30.530
So the goal is to avoid
layoffs at all costs.

00:59:30.530 --> 00:59:31.520
Thank you, Ramona.

00:59:31.520 --> 00:59:34.550
I fully agree with her.

00:59:34.550 --> 00:59:35.060
Excellent.

00:59:35.060 --> 00:59:39.140
I'm sure our community continues
to find that reassuring.

00:59:39.140 --> 00:59:41.240
I want to turn to a
series of questions

00:59:41.240 --> 00:59:46.130
now about students who
are considering perhaps

00:59:46.130 --> 00:59:48.320
taking a gap year
or new students who

00:59:48.320 --> 00:59:50.630
may be considering deferring.

00:59:50.630 --> 00:59:54.830
A lot of questions have
come in about what would we

00:59:54.830 --> 00:59:57.860
say to a student
who is considering

00:59:57.860 --> 00:59:58.730
either one of those.

01:00:01.370 --> 01:00:03.460
What would we say?

01:00:03.460 --> 01:00:06.980
And hear, I know that
Ian and his group

01:00:06.980 --> 01:00:08.610
may want to answer here.

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:09.110
Yes.

01:00:09.110 --> 01:00:12.020
I can see that there
are a lot of people

01:00:12.020 --> 01:00:13.940
would want to jump and
answer that question.

01:00:13.940 --> 01:00:17.030
So Ian, Peko, Cindy--

01:00:17.030 --> 01:00:18.035
who wants to take that?

01:00:18.035 --> 01:00:20.260
Who wants to be the first?

01:00:20.260 --> 01:00:22.630
I can take it.

01:00:22.630 --> 01:00:24.140
They're fighting already.

01:00:24.140 --> 01:00:25.870
Go ahead, Ian.

01:00:25.870 --> 01:00:27.860
I can share at least
the positive news

01:00:27.860 --> 01:00:33.410
that I think we had a record
undergraduate yield this year.

01:00:33.410 --> 01:00:36.440
And our graduate yield is very
high, too, so a lot of people

01:00:36.440 --> 01:00:41.030
are still very excited
about coming to MIT.

01:00:41.030 --> 01:00:43.040
I think the hard
question for everyone

01:00:43.040 --> 01:00:46.820
in thinking about deferrals is
what will the alternatives be

01:00:46.820 --> 01:00:49.670
at that point in time.

01:00:49.670 --> 01:00:52.610
And that's just a very
hard thing to consider.

01:00:52.610 --> 01:00:56.660
But as with most
things at MIT, we

01:00:56.660 --> 01:00:59.600
would do what's in the best
interest of the students.

01:00:59.600 --> 01:01:02.330
I think if the
students felt that it

01:01:02.330 --> 01:01:08.300
was best for them to defer,
we would let them defer.

01:01:08.300 --> 01:01:14.870
So those are the kinds of
things that you know we will

01:01:14.870 --> 01:01:19.280
I think learn more about as
we understand more about what

01:01:19.280 --> 01:01:21.530
the fall will look like--

01:01:21.530 --> 01:01:24.170
on the undergraduate
side, especially.

01:01:24.170 --> 01:01:26.330
But it's a hard
question, because we

01:01:26.330 --> 01:01:29.270
have ideas of what the campus
environment will look like,

01:01:29.270 --> 01:01:32.330
but we don't know what the
alternatives for people

01:01:32.330 --> 01:01:36.020
pursuing internships or travel,
which is likely to be difficult

01:01:36.020 --> 01:01:37.890
still, will be.

01:01:37.890 --> 01:01:42.080
And as I said earlier,
we hope to create still

01:01:42.080 --> 01:01:45.980
a vibrant environment
on campus where students

01:01:45.980 --> 01:01:48.350
get to spend time
with other students

01:01:48.350 --> 01:01:49.940
and learn about great things.

01:01:49.940 --> 01:01:52.905
So a hard question to answer.

01:01:52.905 --> 01:01:53.530
Thank you, Ian.

01:01:53.530 --> 01:01:57.300
Does anybody else want to answer
that question in the panel?

01:02:00.080 --> 01:02:03.410
I'll just second what Ian said,
which is that I am not sure

01:02:03.410 --> 01:02:06.120
that the question is
much different now

01:02:06.120 --> 01:02:07.370
than it is at any time, right?

01:02:07.370 --> 01:02:09.527
At any time, you're
weighing which

01:02:09.527 --> 01:02:11.360
is the option that's
better for the student,

01:02:11.360 --> 01:02:15.060
and I think we would
do the same thing now.

01:02:15.060 --> 01:02:19.240
Well, I think the main point
is that we really, as we

01:02:19.240 --> 01:02:23.170
do every year, try to make it
very attractive and exciting

01:02:23.170 --> 01:02:24.910
for a student to
be engaged at MIT.

01:02:24.910 --> 01:02:28.030
And whether that's on
campus so maybe some time

01:02:28.030 --> 01:02:32.480
away from campus, we
will offer our students

01:02:32.480 --> 01:02:34.060
an experience that
they will never

01:02:34.060 --> 01:02:35.700
forget that is very much MIT.

01:02:35.700 --> 01:02:37.840
That is for certain.

01:02:37.840 --> 01:02:39.880
Alfred, you got anymore?

01:02:39.880 --> 01:02:41.560
Yes, I have one or
two more, Rafael.

01:02:41.560 --> 01:02:43.810
And again, I'm picking
questions or my group

01:02:43.810 --> 01:02:46.990
is picking questions that
are most frequently asked.

01:02:46.990 --> 01:02:50.230
Here's another one that
I think is related.

01:02:50.230 --> 01:02:52.960
It says, transitioning
to college is hard.

01:02:52.960 --> 01:02:56.830
Doing it online
would be even harder.

01:02:56.830 --> 01:02:59.080
How would you support
first year students

01:02:59.080 --> 01:03:01.270
if we're still doing
remote teaching,

01:03:01.270 --> 01:03:05.260
even if we're on campus
while we're doing it?

01:03:05.260 --> 01:03:08.590
Ian, I think this is a
great opportunity for us

01:03:08.590 --> 01:03:11.500
to share a little bit of what
their students are telling us

01:03:11.500 --> 01:03:14.050
from their online
experience, if you want.

01:03:14.050 --> 01:03:17.180
You or Sanjay can do that.

01:03:17.180 --> 01:03:20.530
Or maybe, since I expect
he has a lot of insight

01:03:20.530 --> 01:03:25.330
into how our students are
reaching out both at CPW,

01:03:25.330 --> 01:03:29.590
but also planning for fall
events, David Singer, I expect,

01:03:29.590 --> 01:03:31.570
could give us a real
boots on the ground

01:03:31.570 --> 01:03:35.920
kind of a view about
how our students hope

01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:38.180
to welcome other students.

01:03:38.180 --> 01:03:39.710
David, you're [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:39.710 --> 01:03:41.950
Yeah, I'm happy to
speak about that.

01:03:41.950 --> 01:03:45.280
I think that our students are
already creatively reaching

01:03:45.280 --> 01:03:48.220
out and trying to maintain
a sense of community

01:03:48.220 --> 01:03:51.910
even without the benefit
of physical presence.

01:03:51.910 --> 01:03:55.600
And so the student governance
process still continues.

01:03:55.600 --> 01:03:57.670
The meetings still
continue there

01:03:57.670 --> 01:04:00.730
was a lot of efforts to reach
out to students during CPW

01:04:00.730 --> 01:04:04.750
that that went really well, and
was just as creative, and as

01:04:04.750 --> 01:04:08.980
quirky, and as quintessentially
MIT as it always is.

01:04:08.980 --> 01:04:10.910
And so I expect that's
going to continue.

01:04:10.910 --> 01:04:14.553
And so if we're in a difficult
situation in the fall, where

01:04:14.553 --> 01:04:16.720
there's still distance, and
there still students who

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:20.350
are not on campus, then
the upper class students

01:04:20.350 --> 01:04:23.230
will reach out to them and
form a sense of community,

01:04:23.230 --> 01:04:25.780
so that when we finally
do reunite on campus,

01:04:25.780 --> 01:04:29.410
it's going to feel like
there actually was continuity

01:04:29.410 --> 01:04:31.480
and community building.

01:04:31.480 --> 01:04:32.520
Thank you, David.

01:04:32.520 --> 01:04:33.395
Thanks.

01:04:33.395 --> 01:04:34.760
COVID-19?

01:04:34.760 --> 01:04:36.310
I just want to see
if anyone wants

01:04:36.310 --> 01:04:39.060
to add to that about the
support for first year students,

01:04:39.060 --> 01:04:40.810
if there's anything
else on anyone's mind,

01:04:40.810 --> 01:04:42.542
just before we go on.

01:04:42.542 --> 01:04:44.500
Because I'm going to
shift gears now to housing

01:04:44.500 --> 01:04:47.130
if there's nothing more
to be said on that.

01:04:47.130 --> 01:04:49.410
OK, great.

01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:54.270
So the question is, several
things-- and Suzy Nelson,

01:04:54.270 --> 01:04:56.970
maybe this comes back to you--

01:04:56.970 --> 01:05:00.390
having to do with when
will we announce decisions

01:05:00.390 --> 01:05:04.080
about housing
assignments, number one.

01:05:04.080 --> 01:05:08.300
Number two, are we looking at
the fraternities and sororities

01:05:08.300 --> 01:05:10.050
and other independent
living arrangements,

01:05:10.050 --> 01:05:12.183
and how do they
factor into the mix?

01:05:12.183 --> 01:05:14.100
And the third question
is, what's the thinking

01:05:14.100 --> 01:05:17.220
around fall sports?

01:05:17.220 --> 01:05:18.060
This is all Suzy.

01:05:21.700 --> 01:05:24.440
Thank you, Alfred,
for that question.

01:05:24.440 --> 01:05:26.610
So the first one
about assignments.

01:05:26.610 --> 01:05:30.790
We don't really want to
start making assignments

01:05:30.790 --> 01:05:33.490
until we know what
the program will be--

01:05:33.490 --> 01:05:34.900
the academic program will be.

01:05:34.900 --> 01:05:37.540
So we have a lot of
different models.

01:05:37.540 --> 01:05:40.640
We had a good conversation
with DormCon last week.

01:05:40.640 --> 01:05:42.670
And one of the things
we talked about--

01:05:42.670 --> 01:05:44.590
the students suggested
that perhaps we

01:05:44.590 --> 01:05:46.780
would do-- this goes back
to supporting first year

01:05:46.780 --> 01:05:49.020
students-- that
maybe we would do

01:05:49.020 --> 01:05:52.810
a virtual assignment
for new students

01:05:52.810 --> 01:05:54.832
based on their preference.

01:05:54.832 --> 01:05:56.290
So that would be
a very nice thing,

01:05:56.290 --> 01:05:59.080
because then upperclassmen
could connect right away

01:05:59.080 --> 01:06:03.220
with new students,
depending upon where they

01:06:03.220 --> 01:06:05.440
get assigned to their house.

01:06:05.440 --> 01:06:08.200
So more to come on
assignments and when

01:06:08.200 --> 01:06:09.730
that will all roll out.

01:06:09.730 --> 01:06:11.980
And it will probably
align with when

01:06:11.980 --> 01:06:15.880
a decision is made about when
we start and how we start,

01:06:15.880 --> 01:06:17.920
and how many people
will be back.

01:06:17.920 --> 01:06:19.810
And that relates
to the second part

01:06:19.810 --> 01:06:22.240
of your question, which was
fraternities, sororities,

01:06:22.240 --> 01:06:24.160
and independent living groups.

01:06:24.160 --> 01:06:28.510
They are a very important part
of our experience on campus.

01:06:28.510 --> 01:06:31.780
And we absolutely are
thinking about the FSILGs

01:06:31.780 --> 01:06:35.590
and how they will be linked
in with the housing process.

01:06:35.590 --> 01:06:39.640
We house about 1,100 students in
our FSILGs colleges ordinarily.

01:06:39.640 --> 01:06:42.070
So they're very important
for our housing,

01:06:42.070 --> 01:06:44.860
but they're also very important
for just the social life

01:06:44.860 --> 01:06:45.780
on campus--

01:06:45.780 --> 01:06:50.020
the student social life and
organizational involvement

01:06:50.020 --> 01:06:51.490
that students have.

01:06:51.490 --> 01:06:54.310
And then about athletics,
no real decision

01:06:54.310 --> 01:06:57.370
has been made with athletics.

01:06:57.370 --> 01:07:02.080
The NCAA, they're looking
to advise schools.

01:07:02.080 --> 01:07:05.560
And our conference, we
formed a working group

01:07:05.560 --> 01:07:08.650
to think about this and
what it means for MIT

01:07:08.650 --> 01:07:10.180
and members of our conference.

01:07:10.180 --> 01:07:13.440
But decisions have
not been made yet.

01:07:13.440 --> 01:07:15.102
Thank you, Suzy.

01:07:15.102 --> 01:07:16.380
Alfred?

01:07:16.380 --> 01:07:19.830
I guess the next one,
Rafael, is how are we

01:07:19.830 --> 01:07:22.620
going to support staff who
feel uncomfortable returning

01:07:22.620 --> 01:07:24.870
to on campus work,
but who may feel

01:07:24.870 --> 01:07:28.770
peer pressure or direct
pressure to return to campus?

01:07:28.770 --> 01:07:32.310
What's our overall
approach to supporting

01:07:32.310 --> 01:07:35.460
staff in a gradual
transition back to campus--

01:07:35.460 --> 01:07:37.830
both those who are eager
to come and those who may

01:07:37.830 --> 01:07:39.990
be a little bit more concerned?

01:07:39.990 --> 01:07:41.830
Ramona, you want to
take a crack at it?

01:07:41.830 --> 01:07:43.380
Sure.

01:07:43.380 --> 01:07:47.280
So for people who are
concerned, our recommendation

01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:51.420
is always talk to your
manager or your supervisor.

01:07:51.420 --> 01:07:55.170
And we can hear what those
concerns are and then make

01:07:55.170 --> 01:07:57.300
some adjustments accordingly.

01:07:57.300 --> 01:08:00.270
The goal is not to
have anybody come back

01:08:00.270 --> 01:08:02.520
and feel uncomfortable about it.

01:08:02.520 --> 01:08:05.490
I think they'll there could
be some level of uncertainty

01:08:05.490 --> 01:08:06.810
when we come back.

01:08:06.810 --> 01:08:09.390
It's going to feel new for
us coming back to campus

01:08:09.390 --> 01:08:12.030
after being away for so
long, and then coming back

01:08:12.030 --> 01:08:13.560
in a phased way.

01:08:13.560 --> 01:08:15.660
But I think if you
talk this through

01:08:15.660 --> 01:08:18.960
with your local
administration, and if they

01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:21.689
need to consult with human
resources, they should do that.

01:08:21.689 --> 01:08:24.120
And I'm sure we'll come up
with a supportive plan that

01:08:24.120 --> 01:08:27.029
will make people feel as
comfortable as possible

01:08:27.029 --> 01:08:29.511
under the circumstances.

01:08:29.511 --> 01:08:30.605
Thank you, Ramona.

01:08:30.605 --> 01:08:33.220
You're welcome.

01:08:33.220 --> 01:08:35.740
Here's lot more, Rafael.

01:08:35.740 --> 01:08:38.180
Maybe Sanjay has something
to contribute here

01:08:38.180 --> 01:08:40.359
or maybe you do, too.

01:08:40.359 --> 01:08:42.880
And this is a
question about what

01:08:42.880 --> 01:08:45.640
we're learning from
other countries who

01:08:45.640 --> 01:08:49.840
may be further along on the
timeline of reopening college

01:08:49.840 --> 01:08:51.658
and university campuses.

01:08:51.658 --> 01:08:53.950
And are we in touch with
them, and what are we hearing,

01:08:53.950 --> 01:08:58.076
and how are we integrating
that into our thinking?

01:08:58.076 --> 01:09:00.350
Sanjay, you want to
take a stab at it?

01:09:00.350 --> 01:09:04.160
Maybe I'd like to bounce the
question to Ian, because MIT

01:09:04.160 --> 01:09:07.880
did reach out to a number of
universities around the world

01:09:07.880 --> 01:09:09.149
and get some feedback.

01:09:09.149 --> 01:09:10.609
But Ian has been
collecting that,

01:09:10.609 --> 01:09:14.140
because he's trying to put that
into our thinking for the fall.

01:09:14.140 --> 01:09:15.580
Ian?

01:09:15.580 --> 01:09:16.080
Sure.

01:09:16.080 --> 01:09:18.189
Actually, the person
who is collecting

01:09:18.189 --> 01:09:21.910
is Richard Lester, who did a
marvelous job crowdsourcing it

01:09:21.910 --> 01:09:23.410
through a lot of
faculty members.

01:09:23.410 --> 01:09:27.640
So we have lots of information--

01:09:27.640 --> 01:09:29.200
again, a boots on
the ground kind

01:09:29.200 --> 01:09:31.930
of a look at different
universities.

01:09:31.930 --> 01:09:34.870
And then through
Rafael's efforts,

01:09:34.870 --> 01:09:38.109
we've begun to establish
some discussions

01:09:38.109 --> 01:09:41.200
with some universities
at a higher level

01:09:41.200 --> 01:09:43.149
from around the world
and see what we can

01:09:43.149 --> 01:09:47.080
learn a little bit in advance--

01:09:47.080 --> 01:09:49.210
to sort of see how things
are going as they begin

01:09:49.210 --> 01:09:50.649
to repopulate their campus.

01:09:50.649 --> 01:09:53.770
So the short answer is,
yes, we are reaching out

01:09:53.770 --> 01:09:58.480
to individuals at
Rafael's level, of course,

01:09:58.480 --> 01:10:00.550
at universities--

01:10:00.550 --> 01:10:02.200
leadership-- to
understand how things

01:10:02.200 --> 01:10:04.348
are going-- what their
practices and policies are.

01:10:04.348 --> 01:10:05.890
And what we're
learning, I would say,

01:10:05.890 --> 01:10:08.590
is exactly what Bruce
and Tyler and others

01:10:08.590 --> 01:10:13.450
said, which is masks, masks,
masks, social distancing.

01:10:13.450 --> 01:10:16.780
Phased-- very careful,
very thoughtful.

01:10:16.780 --> 01:10:19.570
And most of them-- most of the
other universities that have

01:10:19.570 --> 01:10:22.450
given us information-- are
in the very early stages--

01:10:22.450 --> 01:10:23.350
baby steps.

01:10:25.890 --> 01:10:32.490
Yeah, I think that the masks
is absolutely the key here.

01:10:32.490 --> 01:10:35.440
I was seeking was somebody
in Guangzhou this morning.

01:10:35.440 --> 01:10:37.650
The university there, which
is very close to Wuhan,

01:10:37.650 --> 01:10:43.350
and had the surge go through
that city, they've opened up

01:10:43.350 --> 01:10:45.900
and they're using
temperature monitoring also

01:10:45.900 --> 01:10:47.640
as a way to try
and identify those

01:10:47.640 --> 01:10:55.890
that are a sentinel
feature of a new infection.

01:10:55.890 --> 01:10:59.310
But the reality is
they they're not

01:10:59.310 --> 01:11:03.300
seeing a resurgence so
far in those communities

01:11:03.300 --> 01:11:04.590
with those implementations.

01:11:04.590 --> 01:11:07.440
So that also, I
think, makes us all

01:11:07.440 --> 01:11:13.540
feel more competent that
that's a good way forward.

01:11:13.540 --> 01:11:14.040
Yes.

01:11:14.040 --> 01:11:16.970
And the only thing that
I can add at high level,

01:11:16.970 --> 01:11:19.880
but we are really collecting
all the information,

01:11:19.880 --> 01:11:21.933
as you heard from others.

01:11:21.933 --> 01:11:24.350
The only other point that I
can note that hasn't been said

01:11:24.350 --> 01:11:28.760
is that in many labs, social
distancing is also being

01:11:28.760 --> 01:11:31.900
translated into a timeshare.

01:11:31.900 --> 01:11:35.160
So there are shifts to work
in the lab, whether it's

01:11:35.160 --> 01:11:37.090
days and evenings
or eight hours,

01:11:37.090 --> 01:11:39.910
or whether it's a Monday,
Wednesday, Friday, Tuesday,

01:11:39.910 --> 01:11:41.240
Thursday, Saturday.

01:11:41.240 --> 01:11:43.620
I mean, there are all sorts
of different variations

01:11:43.620 --> 01:11:46.910
on how to handle the activity.

01:11:46.910 --> 01:11:50.000
In many labs, the
density is pretty

01:11:50.000 --> 01:11:52.220
high in terms of interaction.

01:11:52.220 --> 01:11:55.970
And what I've seen
done, and I have not

01:11:55.970 --> 01:11:59.580
seen that separated--
facemask always present.

01:11:59.580 --> 01:12:04.790
But also, much more separation
among people in laboratories.

01:12:04.790 --> 01:12:07.790
But anyway, we're collecting
all that information.

01:12:07.790 --> 01:12:09.760
Nothing that we have heard--

01:12:09.760 --> 01:12:12.500
and from what I have I read--

01:12:12.500 --> 01:12:14.990
I have not seen the whole
very nicely organized--

01:12:14.990 --> 01:12:17.510
but the feedback that
I've gotten directly,

01:12:17.510 --> 01:12:21.140
that I've read, is everything
consistent with what

01:12:21.140 --> 01:12:24.040
we hear from Bruce and
Tyler and all other experts.

01:12:24.040 --> 01:12:27.560
So it's all trying
to do the same thing.

01:12:27.560 --> 01:12:31.400
What we are trying to get in
addition to what we are doing

01:12:31.400 --> 01:12:32.780
is the results they are getting.

01:12:32.780 --> 01:12:36.110
So you just heard Bruce
describing how at Brigham

01:12:36.110 --> 01:12:40.230
and Women's, they have
a particular database

01:12:40.230 --> 01:12:41.220
we can learn from.

01:12:41.220 --> 01:12:42.950
So I think that
part is also, I'm

01:12:42.950 --> 01:12:46.160
trying to understand so I
can compare, we can compare,

01:12:46.160 --> 01:12:49.400
how effective some
institutions are applying

01:12:49.400 --> 01:12:53.270
the same kinds of safety
procedures or protocols

01:12:53.270 --> 01:12:55.250
than others.

01:12:55.250 --> 01:12:57.210
Alfred, how are we doing?

01:12:57.210 --> 01:12:58.040
We're doing well.

01:12:58.040 --> 01:12:59.870
I just want to let
folks know that we're

01:12:59.870 --> 01:13:02.300
coming toward the
end of the time

01:13:02.300 --> 01:13:04.780
we advertised, but I think
we're going to run it

01:13:04.780 --> 01:13:06.470
a few extra minutes
here just so we can

01:13:06.470 --> 01:13:07.970
cover a couple extra questions.

01:13:07.970 --> 01:13:10.700
And then I know we have
a story we want to share

01:13:10.700 --> 01:13:12.260
with the community as we wrap.

01:13:12.260 --> 01:13:17.180
But let me just get a
few quick lightning round

01:13:17.180 --> 01:13:19.290
questions in, if I
could, and hopefully, we

01:13:19.290 --> 01:13:20.880
can get through these quickly.

01:13:20.880 --> 01:13:23.780
Here's a question for
Ramona about child care.

01:13:23.780 --> 01:13:28.010
Is MIT teeth thinking about
supporting child care options

01:13:28.010 --> 01:13:31.350
as we begin reopening in
the late summer and fall?

01:13:31.350 --> 01:13:33.830
So we're examining
all options right now.

01:13:33.830 --> 01:13:35.660
We're looking for ways
to make enhancements

01:13:35.660 --> 01:13:39.380
to the childcare benefits
that we currently have.

01:13:39.380 --> 01:13:41.810
And we can't do anything
with daycare centers

01:13:41.810 --> 01:13:45.420
until the governor
lifts the restriction.

01:13:45.420 --> 01:13:47.910
But we have a lot of people
thinking about that issue,

01:13:47.910 --> 01:13:48.410
as well.

01:13:48.410 --> 01:13:52.520
We know the importance of
this, especially for faculty

01:13:52.520 --> 01:13:55.670
to come back and teach
online, if children aren't

01:13:55.670 --> 01:13:57.800
at camps or daycare centers.

01:13:57.800 --> 01:14:00.770
So we're examining all
of those possibilities

01:14:00.770 --> 01:14:03.510
and trying to be
creative on top of it.

01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:04.010
Super.

01:14:04.010 --> 01:14:05.360
Thank you, Ramona.

01:14:05.360 --> 01:14:06.950
Sanjay, one for,
you because I know

01:14:06.950 --> 01:14:09.470
you've been thinking about how
to bring the voices of alumni

01:14:09.470 --> 01:14:12.530
into your planning
for 2021 and beyond.

01:14:12.530 --> 01:14:14.120
And that is a
question from alumni.

01:14:14.120 --> 01:14:17.060
Well they have a chance to
offer insights and advice as you

01:14:17.060 --> 01:14:19.130
do your work with [INAUDIBLE]?

01:14:19.130 --> 01:14:19.730
Absolutely.

01:14:19.730 --> 01:14:22.580
I think we're all trying to
figure out a new normal here.

01:14:22.580 --> 01:14:24.710
And there's been
references to crystal ball,

01:14:24.710 --> 01:14:26.720
and the community
as the crystal ball.

01:14:26.720 --> 01:14:30.860
And our alums will be
advisors to this task force.

01:14:30.860 --> 01:14:32.840
So Rick and I
welcome their advice.

01:14:32.840 --> 01:14:35.870
In fact, we will set
up an advisory board

01:14:35.870 --> 01:14:38.150
for the task force for months.

01:14:39.160 --> 01:14:39.937
Fantastic.

01:14:39.937 --> 01:14:41.770
Krystyn, a lightning
round question for you.

01:14:41.770 --> 01:14:45.930
Will there be a facility
for self-isolation on campus

01:14:45.930 --> 01:14:50.290
if we have faculty, staff,
students who get sick?

01:14:50.290 --> 01:14:51.290
That's a great question.

01:14:51.290 --> 01:14:55.540
So we have that facility now
for the folks who are on campus.

01:14:55.540 --> 01:14:57.520
And we do think
that there will be

01:14:57.520 --> 01:15:01.740
for a time waves of infection or
at least the potential for ways

01:15:01.740 --> 01:15:02.830
of infection in the area.

01:15:02.830 --> 01:15:06.370
So we are working on plans
to have isolation facilities

01:15:06.370 --> 01:15:09.250
as part of caring for
the resident community.

01:15:09.250 --> 01:15:12.640
Just as a reminder, we have that
for the resident community now

01:15:12.640 --> 01:15:14.710
as well as the
Collier Care Center

01:15:14.710 --> 01:15:16.970
for folks who aren't part
of the resident community,

01:15:16.970 --> 01:15:18.610
but are part of
the MIT community,

01:15:18.610 --> 01:15:20.170
and need that isolation.

01:15:20.170 --> 01:15:21.280
So it's a resource now.

01:15:21.280 --> 01:15:24.630
It's a resource that we will
continue in our planning.

01:15:24.630 --> 01:15:25.673
Thank you, Krystyn.

01:15:25.673 --> 01:15:27.840
Rafael, I'm going to come
to you in just one second,

01:15:27.840 --> 01:15:29.790
but there's one
question I want to make

01:15:29.790 --> 01:15:31.710
sure we addressed for Suzy.

01:15:31.710 --> 01:15:34.380
And that is there's a
few students who tell us

01:15:34.380 --> 01:15:36.930
that they're struggling
with mental health

01:15:36.930 --> 01:15:38.070
in this difficult time.

01:15:38.070 --> 01:15:44.300
And what advice can we offer
and how can they gain support?

01:15:44.300 --> 01:15:45.620
Thank you for that question.

01:15:45.620 --> 01:15:50.260
So I think what's most
important is don't suffer alone.

01:15:50.260 --> 01:15:53.480
Please reach out to us, because
we are absolutely here for you.

01:15:53.480 --> 01:15:56.110
All of our resources
are available online.

01:15:56.110 --> 01:15:57.670
And there is a
great website which

01:15:57.670 --> 01:16:02.020
we will post after this
session so that you

01:16:02.020 --> 01:16:04.400
can have easy access to it.

01:16:04.400 --> 01:16:06.880
The other thing I
would say is make

01:16:06.880 --> 01:16:09.910
sure you're doing all those
things, those commonsense kind

01:16:09.910 --> 01:16:13.150
of things, to take
care of yourself.

01:16:13.150 --> 01:16:16.600
You know, eating right,
getting enough sleep,

01:16:16.600 --> 01:16:20.950
taking a break from Zoom,
getting outdoors, if you can.

01:16:20.950 --> 01:16:24.520
And doing something
positive for yourself.

01:16:24.520 --> 01:16:26.530
And I guess the last
thing I would say

01:16:26.530 --> 01:16:28.750
is what I have been
doing is thinking

01:16:28.750 --> 01:16:31.420
a lot about what
is right before me

01:16:31.420 --> 01:16:36.250
that I can control and do
today, and not worrying so much

01:16:36.250 --> 01:16:38.650
about those things
that are unknowns,

01:16:38.650 --> 01:16:42.020
and I can't really
do much about today.

01:16:42.020 --> 01:16:44.350
So it's kind of like the
song at the very beginning

01:16:44.350 --> 01:16:48.160
of the session-- pick yourself
up and start all over again.

01:16:48.160 --> 01:16:51.970
I think it's been very tough
for many of us to do that,

01:16:51.970 --> 01:16:55.400
because it's been a
very challenging time.

01:16:55.400 --> 01:16:56.870
So please reach out.

01:16:56.870 --> 01:17:02.230
There are many who can
support you right now.

01:17:02.230 --> 01:17:04.760
Thank you so much, Suzy.

01:17:04.760 --> 01:17:07.540
Just to note, we've
had over 500 questions.

01:17:07.540 --> 01:17:10.150
And one of the
final ones, Rafael,

01:17:10.150 --> 01:17:12.850
is for you, and very
much on the same theme.

01:17:12.850 --> 01:17:17.500
How do you stay positive
during this crisis?

01:17:17.500 --> 01:17:19.480
Well, I appreciate
that question.

01:17:19.480 --> 01:17:22.000
I'm surprised by
it, but thank you.

01:17:22.000 --> 01:17:25.240
Look, I think there is a
little suffering out there.

01:17:25.240 --> 01:17:27.460
Let me just start with that
because that feeling very

01:17:27.460 --> 01:17:28.330
strongly.

01:17:28.330 --> 01:17:30.850
I know, like I'm
sure many of you

01:17:30.850 --> 01:17:32.890
know people who have lost jobs.

01:17:32.890 --> 01:17:35.080
Not at MIT, but elsewhere.

01:17:35.080 --> 01:17:38.140
I know people who
have lost loved ones.

01:17:38.140 --> 01:17:40.360
So there is a lot of
suffering out there,

01:17:40.360 --> 01:17:42.090
and that can bring you down.

01:17:42.090 --> 01:17:43.930
But MIT is a fantastic place.

01:17:43.930 --> 01:17:48.190
I'm just simply thrilled
and fortunate that I

01:17:48.190 --> 01:17:51.130
work in a place in
which it's uplifting.

01:17:51.130 --> 01:17:53.482
Everybody sees a problem,
they want to fix.

01:17:53.482 --> 01:17:54.940
They want to do
something about it.

01:17:54.940 --> 01:17:58.690
When this thing
started, the community

01:17:58.690 --> 01:18:01.190
just got up and tried to
figure out how to help.

01:18:01.190 --> 01:18:03.200
And I want to come back
to that in a moment.

01:18:03.200 --> 01:18:06.050
So it's a place where
I can-do attitude.

01:18:06.050 --> 01:18:07.580
It's a place that is optimistic.

01:18:07.580 --> 01:18:11.260
It's a place that believes
that intellectual brainpower,

01:18:11.260 --> 01:18:15.070
education, science, technology
can make a difference.

01:18:15.070 --> 01:18:17.410
And that just keeps
you going every day.

01:18:17.410 --> 01:18:19.900
And that's just part
of the DNA of MIT.

01:18:19.900 --> 01:18:23.080
I'm just very lucky to be here.

01:18:23.080 --> 01:18:23.830
Thank you, Rafael.

01:18:23.830 --> 01:18:27.520
And so I think that brings
us to the end of the Q&amp;A.

01:18:27.520 --> 01:18:29.740
And I just want
to remind everyone

01:18:29.740 --> 01:18:33.070
that we had such a number
of terrific questions.

01:18:33.070 --> 01:18:35.890
We will be trying to answer
many of these questions

01:18:35.890 --> 01:18:40.660
online on the COVID-19
pages at the MIT website.

01:18:40.660 --> 01:18:43.090
So we'll let you know when
we've entered many more

01:18:43.090 --> 01:18:44.490
of your excellent questions.

01:18:44.490 --> 01:18:46.510
But I think we got to
everything that was

01:18:46.510 --> 01:18:49.910
asked multiple times, Rafael.

01:18:49.910 --> 01:18:51.980
So thank you and the
entire panel for that.

01:18:51.980 --> 01:18:53.800
And now, Rafael, back to you.

01:18:53.800 --> 01:18:54.560
Thank you, Alfred.

01:18:54.560 --> 01:18:58.790
So this is actually, another
way you answer the question

01:18:58.790 --> 01:19:00.770
you asked me, Alfred.

01:19:00.770 --> 01:19:03.268
I want to just spend
just a couple of minutes

01:19:03.268 --> 01:19:04.810
with the help of
our faculty members,

01:19:04.810 --> 01:19:07.330
showing how he might be
has responded to such

01:19:07.330 --> 01:19:10.660
a difficult,
difficult situation.

01:19:10.660 --> 01:19:13.420
As you heard earlier, we
have more than 60 faculty

01:19:13.420 --> 01:19:16.660
that are involved in all
sorts of efforts in response

01:19:16.660 --> 01:19:19.030
to COVID-19, whether
it's providing

01:19:19.030 --> 01:19:23.260
lifesaving medical
supplies, advanced testing.

01:19:23.260 --> 01:19:27.190
Bruce mention about developing
new therapies and treatments

01:19:27.190 --> 01:19:29.020
and even advancing vaccines.

01:19:29.020 --> 01:19:30.998
And some of them even
working in cooperation

01:19:30.998 --> 01:19:32.290
with Bruce and the [INAUDIBLE].

01:19:32.290 --> 01:19:36.580
So it's an amazing place-- how
very quickly people figure out,

01:19:36.580 --> 01:19:38.390
I know how to do
this, I can help,

01:19:38.390 --> 01:19:41.200
even if that's not what they
were doing for research.

01:19:41.200 --> 01:19:43.690
But I want to just
highlight one of them.

01:19:43.690 --> 01:19:46.100
It's an amazing
colleague of ours.

01:19:46.100 --> 01:19:47.200
His name is Simon Johnson.

01:19:47.200 --> 01:19:50.220
He's a professor at MIT
Sloan School of Management.

01:19:50.220 --> 01:19:52.330
And he has been
part of an effort

01:19:52.330 --> 01:19:57.410
to help nursing homes across
the state to face the crisis.

01:19:57.410 --> 01:20:02.110
So Simon, what can you tell us
about what you've been doing?

01:20:02.110 --> 01:20:02.860
Thank you, Rafael.

01:20:02.860 --> 01:20:08.230
Well, as you just mentioned,
we went all in on COVID-19,

01:20:08.230 --> 01:20:10.930
like many of our colleagues,
and many people who are

01:20:10.930 --> 01:20:13.680
listening to this broadcast.

01:20:13.680 --> 01:20:15.790
Our focus, we decided
about eight weeks ago,

01:20:15.790 --> 01:20:18.280
was to try and help nursing
homes, assisted living,

01:20:18.280 --> 01:20:22.030
and other senior care withstand
this incredible and dangerous

01:20:22.030 --> 01:20:23.708
surge of COVID-19.

01:20:23.708 --> 01:20:25.750
And we worked long and
hard to try and figure out

01:20:25.750 --> 01:20:26.830
how to do this.

01:20:26.830 --> 01:20:28.870
And about five weeks
ago, I had a meeting

01:20:28.870 --> 01:20:30.910
with the head of the
Massachusetts Senior Care

01:20:30.910 --> 01:20:32.930
Association, Tara Gregorio.

01:20:32.930 --> 01:20:34.930
And I thought at that
point, we'd learned a lot,

01:20:34.930 --> 01:20:37.240
and we were quite
expert, in my view,

01:20:37.240 --> 01:20:40.660
about what we could provide
and what nursing homes needed.

01:20:40.660 --> 01:20:42.640
And Tara said to me,
you know, Simon, there's

01:20:42.640 --> 01:20:45.710
one thing we need and really,
only one thing right now,

01:20:45.710 --> 01:20:47.230
and that's more testing.

01:20:47.230 --> 01:20:49.517
And at that moment, I
said to myself, that's

01:20:49.517 --> 01:20:50.600
the one thing we can't do.

01:20:50.600 --> 01:20:52.142
I don't know how to
get more testing.

01:20:52.142 --> 01:20:54.060
And fortunately, my
colleague, [INAUDIBLE],,

01:20:54.060 --> 01:20:56.070
who is the brains
behind this and had

01:20:56.070 --> 01:20:58.150
a lot of the original
ideas, said to me, Simon,

01:20:58.150 --> 01:20:59.590
call up the Broad
Institute, and see if we

01:20:59.590 --> 01:21:00.900
can get some of their tests.

01:21:00.900 --> 01:21:03.240
And they did
provide those tests.

01:21:03.240 --> 01:21:06.060
And we connected them
with Mass Senior Care.

01:21:06.060 --> 01:21:07.408
And then we needed test tubes.

01:21:07.408 --> 01:21:08.700
So where did we get test tubes?

01:21:08.700 --> 01:21:11.280
Where did we get the test
kits to make this happen?

01:21:11.280 --> 01:21:13.000
MIT Medical came through for us.

01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:14.700
And again and
again, this has been

01:21:14.700 --> 01:21:17.670
what's our experience, Rafael,
which is we work the problem,

01:21:17.670 --> 01:21:19.890
we try to be smart
about it-- we are MIT.

01:21:19.890 --> 01:21:21.768
And we find colleagues.

01:21:21.768 --> 01:21:24.060
We have more than 10 faculty
at MIT who worked on this.

01:21:24.060 --> 01:21:26.370
We find students-- more than
50 students have helped us

01:21:26.370 --> 01:21:27.780
with some aspect to this.

01:21:27.780 --> 01:21:29.810
We find people all
around campus who

01:21:29.810 --> 01:21:32.790
are willing to give us
ideas or test tubes or swabs

01:21:32.790 --> 01:21:34.320
or autoclave the swabs.

01:21:34.320 --> 01:21:35.880
Whatever it is,
whatever it takes,

01:21:35.880 --> 01:21:38.240
there's always somebody
in the MIT network.

01:21:38.240 --> 01:21:40.140
And then there are
the friends of MIT.

01:21:40.140 --> 01:21:41.905
There are people in
the alumni network.

01:21:41.905 --> 01:21:43.530
There's the Schmidt
Futures Foundation,

01:21:43.530 --> 01:21:45.930
which gave us a key early
grant to make this work happen.

01:21:45.930 --> 01:21:47.430
There's the Biogen
foundation, which

01:21:47.430 --> 01:21:50.060
came through with a big
additional grant just now,

01:21:50.060 --> 01:21:52.560
that has in turn
unlocked, I would

01:21:52.560 --> 01:21:54.900
say, a change in mindset and
understanding at the state

01:21:54.900 --> 01:21:55.430
level.

01:21:55.430 --> 01:21:58.860
And the state has now
provided $130 million

01:21:58.860 --> 01:22:00.780
to help nursing
homes and assisted

01:22:00.780 --> 01:22:03.810
living and other facilities
around the state.

01:22:03.810 --> 01:22:06.450
And now the real work begins,
because the state has provided

01:22:06.450 --> 01:22:08.020
the money, but
what's the strategy?

01:22:08.020 --> 01:22:08.670
How are you going to do it?

01:22:08.670 --> 01:22:10.830
How are you going to take
ideas such as the one put

01:22:10.830 --> 01:22:13.250
forward by Bruce Walker
several times today--

01:22:13.250 --> 01:22:13.800
masks?

01:22:13.800 --> 01:22:16.380
How do you provide
better PPE for the people

01:22:16.380 --> 01:22:17.560
working in nursing homes?

01:22:17.560 --> 01:22:19.620
How do you improve
infection control?

01:22:19.620 --> 01:22:21.720
How do you make sure there
are enough workers who

01:22:21.720 --> 01:22:22.928
are willing and able to work?

01:22:22.928 --> 01:22:25.050
Now I don't have
all the answers,

01:22:25.050 --> 01:22:26.250
but the community does.

01:22:26.250 --> 01:22:28.508
The people around
Massachusetts do.

01:22:28.508 --> 01:22:29.550
We can connect with them.

01:22:29.550 --> 01:22:31.470
We can provide end
to end solutions.

01:22:31.470 --> 01:22:33.690
We can provide all the
logistics necessary to get

01:22:33.690 --> 01:22:36.723
the right experts from
MIT and from other places,

01:22:36.723 --> 01:22:39.390
and other hospitals, and even, I
would say, other universities--

01:22:39.390 --> 01:22:42.450
I use that word carefully--
in the community

01:22:42.450 --> 01:22:45.900
applied to this problem to
help all the residents, and all

01:22:45.900 --> 01:22:48.630
the staff, in every
single nursing home

01:22:48.630 --> 01:22:51.810
and assisted living
in Massachusetts.

01:22:51.810 --> 01:22:53.850
And then, Rafael,
the question is--

01:22:53.850 --> 01:22:56.142
so this question was put to
me by the Biogen Foundation

01:22:56.142 --> 01:22:58.475
people this afternoon-- they
said, this is great, Simon.

01:22:58.475 --> 01:22:59.520
You're making progress.

01:22:59.520 --> 01:23:02.910
But when can we take this
model and these ideas

01:23:02.910 --> 01:23:04.500
and show it to the
rest of the country

01:23:04.500 --> 01:23:06.640
and help people
everywhere in the United

01:23:06.640 --> 01:23:09.510
States think about how
to protect the older

01:23:09.510 --> 01:23:11.428
members of their
communities, and everyone

01:23:11.428 --> 01:23:13.470
who works with them, and
tries to keep them safe?

01:23:13.470 --> 01:23:14.940
So it doesn't end, Rafael.

01:23:14.940 --> 01:23:16.110
It keeps going.

01:23:16.110 --> 01:23:17.100
It keeps working.

01:23:17.100 --> 01:23:19.770
And thank you, Rafael, and
thank you, everyone here,

01:23:19.770 --> 01:23:21.540
for your leadership
and your inspiration

01:23:21.540 --> 01:23:22.888
in these difficult times.

01:23:22.888 --> 01:23:24.180
Thank you for your inspiration.

01:23:24.180 --> 01:23:26.130
Let me tell that
thousands of people

01:23:26.130 --> 01:23:30.930
watching in the townhall
that beware of Simon Johnson.

01:23:30.930 --> 01:23:32.910
he's a very dangerous man.

01:23:32.910 --> 01:23:36.360
This guy a few weeks ago,
maybe a month ago or so--

01:23:36.360 --> 01:23:37.270
no two months ago--

01:23:37.270 --> 01:23:41.460
I think he called on a Sunday
afternoon at 2 o'clock,

01:23:41.460 --> 01:23:44.340
and he tells me, you got
to give me the governor

01:23:44.340 --> 01:23:45.860
tonight at 7:00 PM.

01:23:45.860 --> 01:23:47.190
I said, Simon, you're crazy.

01:23:47.190 --> 01:23:47.763
What for?

01:23:47.763 --> 01:23:48.930
And he explained that to me.

01:23:48.930 --> 01:23:52.350
And I said, Simon, if every one
of 1,000 faculty members say,

01:23:52.350 --> 01:23:55.160
I want to see the governor,
you think I should call them?

01:23:55.160 --> 01:23:57.360
No, but this is you
have to do that.

01:23:57.360 --> 01:24:00.920
Well, I did, and he showed
up, and he heard the idea,

01:24:00.920 --> 01:24:02.280
and then good things happened.

01:24:02.280 --> 01:24:04.020
So be careful with this guy.

01:24:04.020 --> 01:24:06.330
He doesn't stop.

01:24:06.330 --> 01:24:09.750
I want to tell you all that I
got a letter based on the work

01:24:09.750 --> 01:24:12.840
that Simon did-- a letter a
few days ago from the head

01:24:12.840 --> 01:24:15.900
of the state association
for seniors care.

01:24:15.900 --> 01:24:20.370
And in that letter, he referred
to Simon and his MIT team who

01:24:20.370 --> 01:24:22.950
supported them through
these very dark days

01:24:22.950 --> 01:24:25.200
as our guardian angels.

01:24:25.200 --> 01:24:26.497
So it gives me goosebumps.

01:24:26.497 --> 01:24:28.080
Simon, well, thank
you so much for all

01:24:28.080 --> 01:24:32.640
you're doing for the many
people have just forgotten.

01:24:32.640 --> 01:24:34.200
Thank you so much.

01:24:34.200 --> 01:24:37.920
Well, we're done for the day
in terms of this townhall.

01:24:37.920 --> 01:24:41.155
I want to thank all the
panelists for participating

01:24:41.155 --> 01:24:42.030
and for the openness.

01:24:42.030 --> 01:24:44.430
I want to thank all of
you for participating

01:24:44.430 --> 01:24:46.710
from all over the world.

01:24:46.710 --> 01:24:49.770
As you heard from us, we are
facing difficult decisions.

01:24:49.770 --> 01:24:51.710
They are very complex.

01:24:51.710 --> 01:24:53.315
And we're going to
find ways to invite

01:24:53.315 --> 01:24:54.690
all of you who
want to contribute

01:24:54.690 --> 01:24:56.700
to it offer your ideas.

01:24:56.700 --> 01:24:59.430
I also want to repeat again,
as I started in the beginning,

01:24:59.430 --> 01:25:01.660
that we want to make
this each of us better

01:25:01.660 --> 01:25:05.220
and with respect for everyone's
opinions and experiences

01:25:05.220 --> 01:25:07.440
and with empathy and compassion.

01:25:07.440 --> 01:25:09.330
Let me conclude with
something that I

01:25:09.330 --> 01:25:12.450
said at the end of
the last townhall.

01:25:12.450 --> 01:25:16.970
We may be in 10,000 places,
but we are still one MIT.

01:25:16.970 --> 01:25:21.130
And as they say in the
hometown where I come from,

01:25:21.130 --> 01:25:22.170
hasta la vista.

01:25:22.170 --> 01:25:24.720
Thank you.

01:25:24.720 --> 01:25:26.370
Thank you all, for, joining us.

01:25:26.370 --> 01:25:27.570
And thank you, Rafael.

01:25:27.570 --> 01:25:31.110
Just to remind you-- a recording
will be available at this URL

01:25:31.110 --> 01:25:34.430
shortly for those of you who
can't wait to watch again.

01:25:34.430 --> 01:25:38.160
And please, music
maestro, close us out.

01:25:38.160 --> 01:25:41.850
We Can Work It Out by the
Beatles, John and Paul.

01:27:28.710 --> 01:27:30.550
Thank you, Martin.

